window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"","i":"ccs-105-health-environment-and-society","x":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society University of Embu · Biological Sciences This pack organizes the available course outline and lecture materials into a topic-by-topic study path. Search covers the text inside each note. Use the progress tracker to mark a topic complete only after you can explain it without looking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"Learning goals","i":"learning-goals","x":"Learning goals Work through each topic in order, or search for a specific term or example. Use the interactive recall checklist at the end of each note. Record confidence and revision dates in the progress tracker. Consult the source material when a note marks a gap or an equation needs checking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 01 · Holistic Wellness 02 · Communicable Diseases 03 · Noncommunicable Diseases 04 · Mental Health 05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya 06 · Hygiene and Sanitation 07 · Climate Change 08 · Digital Environment 09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment 10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya"},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — Health, Environment, and Society","h":"How to study","i":"how-to-study","x":"How to study Read one topic and mark its checklist as you complete each activity. Close the notes and write the key ideas from memory. Use search or “Find in note” to resolve specific gaps. Update your confidence and next review date in the progress tracker."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"","i":"your-study-plan","x":"Your study plan"},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Weekly study cycle","i":"weekly-study-cycle","x":"Weekly study cycle Preview the course outline and choose this week's topics. Study one or two notes per session. Do a closed-book recall after each note. Revisit weak topics within 48 hours. Complete a mixed-topic review at the end of the week."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Exam-week cycle","i":"exam-week-cycle","x":"Exam-week cycle Review the progress tracker and prioritize low-confidence topics. Rework examples without looking at their solutions. Search the notes for definitions and distinctions you cannot recall. Complete the question bank and record errors in the tracker. Protect time for sleep and a final light review."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CCS 105 — topic map","h":"","i":"ccs-105-topic-map","x":"CCS 105 — topic map Use this page as a fast index, not a replacement for the notes. Search by definition, method, example, or phrase to jump into the relevant section. 01 · Holistic Wellness — notes/01_holistic-wellness.md 02 · Communicable Diseases — notes/02_communicable-diseases.md 03 · Noncommunicable Diseases — notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.md 04 · Mental Health — notes/04_mental-health.md 05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya — notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.md 06 · Hygiene and Sanitation — notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.md 07 · Climate Change — notes/07_climate-change.md 08 · Digital Environment — notes/08_digital-environment.md 09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment — notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.md 10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya — notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.md"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"","i":"progress-tracker","x":"Progress tracker Mark a topic complete only when you can explain it without the notes. Use the local progress panel to see completed pages. Mark pages as done and they will appear here."},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"Topic mastery","i":"topic-mastery","x":"Topic mastery"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"Topic mastery","i":"topic-mastery-2","x":"Topic mastery 01 · Holistic Wellness — Open note 02 · Communicable Diseases — Open note 03 · Noncommunicable Diseases — Open note 04 · Mental Health — Open note 05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya — Open note 06 · Hygiene and Sanitation — Open note 07 · Climate Change — Open note 08 · Digital Environment — Open note 09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment — Open note 10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya — Open note"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"Progress tracker","h":"Weak-topic action plan","i":"weak-topic-action-plan","x":"Weak-topic action plan Record the topic and what is unclear. Choose the next action and review date. Revisit the topic and raise your confidence score."},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"","i":"01-holistic-wellness","x":"01 · Holistic Wellness Source: Topic 1 Holistic Wellness.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes --- slide 11 --- (g) Environmental wellness Environmental wellness includes the balance between home and work life, as well as the individual's relationship with nature and community resources, ie . involvement in a recycling or community clean-up effort. Further, the importance of safety of food and water supply, infectious diseases, violence, ultraviolet radiation, air and water pollution, and second-hand tobacco smoke also fall under this dimension. City planning can have an effect on human wellness. For example, green space in cities can have a positive effect on health by increasing opportunities for physical activity and also create feelings of relaxation and wellbeing. In addition, vegetation can improve air quality by removing particulates and pollutant --- slide 11 --- (f) Occupational wellness ‘Occupational wellness has been defined as the level of satisfaction and enrichment gained from one's work, whether paid or unpaid, and the extent one's occupation allows for the expression of one's values. Occupational wellness includes the contribution of one's unique skills, talents and services to the community and the level to which the individual views their work"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"pational wellness includes the contribution of one's unique skills, talents and services to the community and the level to which the individual views their work as rewarding and meaningful. Achieving a balance between occupational responsibilities and other commitments is indicative of the level of occupational wellness --- slide 11 --- (h) Economic wellness An analysis of wellness in relation to social capital within approximately 50 countries, examined income, employment, government effectiveness, family structure and social relations in order to assess effects of institutions and policies. Increased income inequality is associated with lower rates of economic growth and poorer health. Individuals attaching high subjective values to financial success have lower values for subjective wellbeing , even when their financial aspirations were met. Higher levels of subjective wellbeing are found not in the richest countries but among those who live where social and political institutions are effective , where mutual trust is high and corruption is low. Studies consistently show a large negative effect of individual unemployment on subjective wellbeing. --- slide 11 --- (j) Climate"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"s high and corruption is low. Studies consistently show a large negative effect of individual unemployment on subjective wellbeing. --- slide 11 --- (j) Climate wellness It is hardly surprising that extreme weather (whether or not it is caused by climate change), is detrimental to subjective wellbeing whether directly or at the community. level from disruptions to social and economic activities. Not only have environmental determinants been shown to negatively affect mental health, but the implications of climate change is also now causing emotional distress, for instance among Australian children 25% of those surveyed believed the world might end in the next few years. --- slide 11 --- ( i ) Cultural wellness Examination of cultural differences in wellness across a number of nations confirms that the cultural environment is an important factor, because there are substantial differences in subjective wellbeing between cultures. --- slide 11 --- (e) Spiritual wellness Spiritual wellness is probably one of the most developed and discussed topic in the literature. It is important to note at the outset that spirituality and religion are not synonymous and the two concepts, while"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"loped and discussed topic in the literature. It is important to note at the outset that spirituality and religion are not synonymous and the two concepts, while overlapping, are entirely distinct from one another. The key aspects of spiritual wellness seem to be purpose and meaning in life; the self in relation to others, the community, nature, the universe, and some higher power; shared community and experience; an inner resource that may give an individual a feeling of strength and become a guide to finding significance in life; and the creation of personal values and believe. --- slide 11 --- Components/Dimensions of holistic wellness (a)Physical wellness This dimension is particularly relevant where cardiovascular fitness, flexibility, and strength are concerned. Actions to improve physical wellness include maintaining a healthy exercise regime and diet and monitoring internal and external physical signs of the body's response to events, including stress. This includes seeking medical care when appropriate and taking action to prevent and avoiding harmful behaviors (e.g. tobacco and excess alcohol use) and detect illnesses. --- slide 11 --- Topic 1: HOLISTIC WELLNESS What is"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"action to prevent and avoiding harmful behaviors (e.g. tobacco and excess alcohol use) and detect illnesses. --- slide 11 --- Topic 1: HOLISTIC WELLNESS What is Holistic Wellness? The word ‘Holistic’ derives from ‘Holism’, with Greek roots ‘Holos’, meaning whole, complete, entire. Wellbeing has become an increasingly prominent topic in both psychological and medical research in the public discourse, as it transcends traditional health paradigms by emphasizing a holistic view of an individual’s mental, physical, social, and spiritual dimensions. --- slide 11 --- (b) Emotional/psychological wellness Explanations of emotional/psychological wellness primarily focus around attitudes and beliefs about self and life. They include the ability to develop a positive and realistic self concept and a sense of purpose in life. In this regard of course, work-life often impacts on this sense of identity, and self-esteem. A positive self-view and awareness of one's feelings will in turn shape actions, relationships and autonomy. So too will having a personal developmental focus that includes notions of self-actualization. --- slide 11 --- (d) Intellectual/cognitive wellness Intellectual/cognitive"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"l having a personal developmental focus that includes notions of self-actualization. --- slide 11 --- (d) Intellectual/cognitive wellness Intellectual/cognitive wellness is the perception of, and motivation for, an individual's optimal level of stimulating intellectual activity by the continual acquisition, use, sharing, and application of knowledge in a creative and critical fashion. This is for both personal growth and the betterment of society. Realizing one's personal potential involves cognitive processes and comprehension of life's purpose. Education increases opportunities for income and job security, and gives people a sense of control over life circumstances. Low levels of educational attainment are less likely to lead to high levels of employment security and thus quality of life may be reduced --- slide 11 --- (c) Social wellness Social wellness is broad in scope because it includes the interaction of the individual with others, the community, and nature. It includes the interaction (quality and extent) with, and support of, others, the community, and the social and natural environment. Besides the interactions of the individual, society, and nature, social wellness"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"and support of, others, the community, and the social and natural environment. Besides the interactions of the individual, society, and nature, social wellness includes the motivation, action, intent, and perception of interactions. The importance of the social determinants when assessing health inequalities has been stressed in a recent report from the World Health Organization"},{"u":"notes/01_holistic-wellness.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Holistic Wellness","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"","i":"02-communicable-diseases","x":"02 · Communicable Diseases Source: TOPIC 2 COMMUNICABLE DISEASES.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes CCS 105: HEALTH, ENVIRONMENT , AND SOCIETY TOPIC 2: COMMUNICABLE DISEASES  A communicable disease is a disease caused by an infectious agent (or pathogen) that can be transmitted from an infected person, animal or environmental source to another susceptible host.  Communicable diseases are also known as infectious or contagious (highly communicable) diseases.  These diseases remain an important public health concern because they can spread rapidly between individuals and within communities. How do communicable diseases spread?  They spread wheninfectious agents, such as bacteria, viruses, parasites or fungi , move from an infected person, animal or environmental sourcetoa susceptibleperson.  Commoninfectiousagents includethefollowing: Modes of transmission  Transmission occurs when an infectious agent reaches a susceptiblehostthrough asuitableroute.  The diseases are spread throughdirect transmission or indirect transmission. A. Direct transmission  Infectious agent passes directly from an infected person or animal (reservoir) to another individual (host). This requires physical closeness or contact. No intermediate objects or creatures are involved.  The two"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"on or animal (reservoir) to another individual (host). This requires physical closeness or contact. No intermediate objects or creatures are involved.  The two primary modes of direct disease transmission aredirect physical contact and droplet spread. A. Direct transmission of communicable diseases Direct physical contact : touching, kissing, sexual contact, skin-to- skin contact. Handling or sharing body fluids (blood, saliva, mucus). Transmissionfrom mother tochild(vertical transmission). Droplet spread : coughing, sneezing, or speaking creates respiratory droplets that travel a short distance and land on another person's face,mouth, or eyes. E.g.,influenza and whooping cough. B. Indirect transmission of communicable diseases  Indirect transmission occurs when infectious agents (pathogens) reach a host through an intermediate route (environment, object, particle, carrier, or living creature).  This may involve contaminated food, water, objects or surfaces, shared objects, airborne particles, blood or medical equipment, living organisms (vectors). Three primary modes of indirect transmission Airborne transmission: Pathogens travel via tiny dried respiratory droplets (droplet"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"t, living organisms (vectors). Three primary modes of indirect transmission Airborne transmission: Pathogens travel via tiny dried respiratory droplets (droplet nuclei) or dust particles that stay suspended in the air for long periods and travel over greater distances. A new host can breathe them in even after the infected person has left the site. E.g.,tuberculosis,measles. Vehicle-borne transmission : Non-living objects or substances (known as fomites or vehicles) carryand transferpathogens. a) Fomites include contaminated inanimate items (doorknobs, phones, toys, clothing, or medical equipment) that a person touches and then touching their own eyes,nose,or mouth. E.g.,commoncold. b) Vehicles/substances included contaminated food, water, or blood products.E.g.,cholera (waterborne) andsalmonella (foodborne). Vector-borne transmission : Living intermediaries, usually insects like mosquitoes, ticks, fleas, or flies, carry pathogens from one host to another. E.g.,malaria spread by mosquitoes. Examples of communicable diseases  Communicable diseases occur in both high- and low-income countries. However, factors such as poverty, inadequate/poor sanitation, limited healthcare access,"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"s  Communicable diseases occur in both high- and low-income countries. However, factors such as poverty, inadequate/poor sanitation, limited healthcare access, malnutrition, inadequate clean water, overcrowding and poor living/working conditions can increase vulnerability tothese diseases.  World Health Organization (WHO) highlights communicable diseases, including HIV/AIDS, TB, malaria, viral hepatitis , sexually transmitted infections (STIs) and neglected tropical diseases (NTDs), among the leading causes of death and disability in low-income countries and marginalized populations.  Human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) is an infection that attacks the body's immune system,specificallythe whiteblood cells(CD4 cells).  If untreated, HIV can progress to AIDS (Acquired immunodeficiency syndrome), themostadvanced stageofHIVinfection. Modes of transmission: HIV can be transmitted via the exchange of body fluids from people living with HIV, including blood, breast milk, semen,and vaginal secretions.The virus is mainly transmittedthrough:  Unprotectedsexual exposure (vaginal or anal).  Sharingcontaminatedneedles or other injectingequipment.  Transmission from mother to child during"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"tedthrough:  Unprotectedsexual exposure (vaginal or anal).  Sharingcontaminatedneedles or other injectingequipment.  Transmission from mother to child during pregnancy, childbirth or breastfeeding.  Not transmitted through casual social interactions (hugging, shaking hands, sharingfood,toilet). HIV/AIDS  Prevention: 1) Condoms use, 2) Regular HIV testing and early treatment, 3) Pre-exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) for people at substantial risk and post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) after possible exposure, 4) Avoid sharing needles or other equipment that may contain blood, 5) Safe blood screening and transfusion practices, 6) Prevention ofmother-to childtransmission.HIV  Treatment: HIV is managed using antiretroviral therapy (ART), which suppresses the virus and allows the immune system to remainstrong.ARTdoes notcureHIV,but when takenconsistentlyit can reduce the viral load to an undetectable level. A person with a sustained undetectable viral load does not transmit HIV sexually (U=U:Undetectable = Untransmittable).  HIV is fully preventable: Effective ART prevents mother-to-child and sexual transmission of HIV . HIV/AIDS  Mosquito-borne infectious diseasecaused"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ittable).  HIV is fully preventable: Effective ART prevents mother-to-child and sexual transmission of HIV . HIV/AIDS  Mosquito-borne infectious diseasecaused byPlasmodiumparasites.  Mainly transmitted through the bite of an infected femaleAnopheles mosquito.Symptomsarefever, chills,headache, weakness,sweating.  Prevention: 1) Use insecticide-treated mosquito nets, 2) Indoor vector control, 3) Reduce mosquito breeding sites/stagnant water, 4) Use mosquito repellents and protective clothing, 5) Appropriate malaria preventive medicines where indicated. 6) Malaria vaccines as prevention strategiesineligible childreninhigh-risk areas.  Treatment: Artemisinin-based combination therapies (ACTs) for uncomplicated malaria. Severe malaria requires urgent medical treatment, usually with injectable antimalarial medicines. Early diagnosis and treatmenthelp prevent severe disease and death.  Malaria is a preventable and treatable disease. Malaria  Infectious disease caused by bacteria (Mycobacterium tuberculosis), and commonly affectingthelungs.  Spread through the air when a person with infectious pulmonary TB coughs, sneezes, speaks or sings, releasing infectious particles. A person"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ffectingthelungs.  Spread through the air when a person with infectious pulmonary TB coughs, sneezes, speaks or sings, releasing infectious particles. A person needs to inhale only a few infectious agents (bacteria) to becomeinfected. Tuberculosis (TB)  TB transmission is more likely in crowded or poorly ventilated spaces.  Not spread through casual socialinteractions.  Prevention: 1) Early detection and diagnosis, 2) Prompt treatment, 3) contact tracing and screening, 4) TB preventive treatment for eligible individuals at high risk, 5) BCG vaccination, particularly for children in countries where TB is common, 6) Good ventilation and respiratory hygiene, 7) Covering themouthand nosewhen coughing or sneezing.  Treatment: TB is treated with a combination of antibiotics, usually taken for several months (standard 6-month course). Completing the full treatment course is essential to prevent drug resistance and relapse.  TB is preventable and curable , although drug-resistant TB remains an important public health challenge. Tuberculosis (TB)  Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is an infectious respiratory disease causedby the SARS-CoV-2virus (coronavirus).  Transmission occurs"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"lenge. Tuberculosis (TB)  Coronavirus disease (COVID-19) is an infectious respiratory disease causedby the SARS-CoV-2virus (coronavirus).  Transmission occurs mainly through infectious respiratory particles released when an infected person breathes, speaks, coughs or sneezes. COVID-19  COVID-19 can range from mild illnesstoseveredisease.  Transmission risk increases during close-contact and poorly ventilatedindoorenvironments.  Prevention: 1) Vaccination, especially for people at increased risk of severe disease, 2) Good ventilation and avoid crowded spaces during outbreaks, 3) Practice good respiratory hygiene and hand hygiene, 4) Use of masks, 5) Stay away, and limit contact with others when experiencing symptoms.  Treatment: 1) Most mild cases require rest, fluids and treatment of symptoms. 2) People at high risk of severe disease may require early antiviral treatment prescribed by a healthcare professional. 3) Severe cases may require hospital care, including oxygen and other supportive treatment. COVID-19  Knowing that communicable diseases can be transmitted, what is your role in preventing their spread ?  Role of individuals : Practice healthy behaviours and good"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"owing that communicable diseases can be transmitted, what is your role in preventing their spread ?  Role of individuals : Practice healthy behaviours and good hygiene, seek testing and early diagnosis, seek healthcare when symptoms occur, follow prescribed treatment, apply prevention measures, avoid spreading infection to others, protect others from respiratory infections,and obtainreliable health information.  Health systems: Educatecommunities,conduct diseasesurveillance, diagnose and treat diseases, provide vaccines and preventive interventions, monitor thediseasetrends,and respond tooutbreaks.  Communities: Promote hygiene and sanitation, support vaccination and prevention programmes, reduce environmental risks, encourage early healthcare seeking, support disease surveillance, reduce stigma associated with diseases such as HIV and TB, and promote accurate healthinformation. Lecture 3: Noncommunicable diseases Next"},{"u":"notes/02_communicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Communicable Diseases","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"","i":"03-noncommunicable-diseases","x":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases Source: TOPIC 3 NONCOMMUNICABLE DISEASES.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes CCS 105: HEALTH, ENVIRONMENT , AND SOCIETY TOPIC 3: NONCOMMUNICABLE DISEASES  Noncommunicable diseases (NCDs) are chronic (lasting for a long time) medical conditions that cannot be spread between individuals.  They are the result of a combination of:  Genetic factors  Physiological factors  Environmental factors  Behavioural factors  Among these, behavioral or lifestyle risk factors play the dominant role. NCDs Core risk factors  Risk factors increase the likelihood of developing a disease.  Two categories of risk factors: Modifiable (can be changed). Non-modifiable (Cannot be changed). The major groups of NCDs include: WHO data:  NCDs cause 74% of all deaths worldwide.  NCDs killed ~43 million people in 2021.  73% in low- & middle- income countries.  The four groups account for 80% of all premature NCD deaths. Other notable NCDs  Mental health disorders : Depression, anxiety disorders & schizophrenia.  Chronic kidney disease: Kidney function gradually declines. Closely associated with diabetes & hypertension.  Oral diseases: Dental caries (tooth decay) & periodontal disease (gum disease).  Musculoskeletal diseases: Osteoarthritis & chronic"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"diabetes & hypertension.  Oral diseases: Dental caries (tooth decay) & periodontal disease (gum disease).  Musculoskeletal diseases: Osteoarthritis & chronic musculoskeletal conditions, causing pain, reduced mobility, disability.  Sickle cell disease:Inherited blood disorder that affects red blood cells, causing anaemia, pain crises & other complications.  Neurological diseases (affect nervous system): Epilepsy, Parkinson's disease, Alzheimer's disease & Dementia. A. Modifiable risk factors  Factors that can potentially be changed through individual or public- health interventions. They include behavioural, physiological (metabolic), and environmental risk factors.  Behavioural risk factors: 1) Tobacco use, including second-hand smoke, 2) Unhealthy diets, including excess salt, sugar, and fats, 3) Harmful use of alcohol harms the liver, 4) Physical inactivity, where lack of regular exercise leads to weight gain and poor heart health.  Environmental risk factors: Air pollution (indoor & outdoor) accounts for 6.7 million deaths globally, of which about 5.6 million are due to NCDs, including stroke, ischaemic heart disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and lung"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"million deaths globally, of which about 5.6 million are due to NCDs, including stroke, ischaemic heart disease, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, and lung cancer. A. Modifiable risk factors  Metabolic risk factors: Behavioural risk factors contribute to four key metabolic changes that increase the risk of NCDs: 1) Raised blood pressure (hypertension), 2) Overweight/obesity, 3) High blood glucose levels (diabetes), 4) Abnormal blood lipids (high cholesterol).  Leading metabolic risk factor globally iselevated blood pressure, followed byraised blood glucose,and overweight & obesity.  Metabolic risk factors cannot be directly manipulated in the way that behavioral factors can, although, with the right strategies, alteration is possible. Singh, A., Banerjee, P., Anas, M., Singh, N., & Qamar, I. (2020). Traditional nutritional and health practices targeting lifestyle behavioral changes in humans. Journal of Lifestyle Medicine, 10(2), 67. Over to you:  MY HEALTH  MY CHOICE  MY FUTURE B. Non-modifiable risk factors  These are factors that cannot easily be changed. They include biological traits and background that you cannot change or control.  Heredity and genetics : Some"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"These are factors that cannot easily be changed. They include biological traits and background that you cannot change or control.  Heredity and genetics : Some individuals inherit genetic characteristics that increase their susceptibility to some NCDs.  A family history of conditions such as diabetes, hypertension or certain cancers may increase an individual's risk.  Age: The risk of many NCDs increases with age because of cumulative exposure to risk factors and physiological changes.  Sex/gender: Some diseases have different levels of occurrence or risk among males and females Diabetes mellitus: Type 2 Diabetes  A chronic metabolic disease in whichblood glucose levels remain abnormally highbecause the body does not produce enough insulin or cannot use insulin effectively.  Risk factors: 1) Overweight or obesity, 2) Physical inactivity, 3) Unhealthy diet, 4) Family history and genetic factors, 5) Increasing age, 5) Gestational diabetes (during pregnancy) significantly increases the risk of developing type 2 diabetes later in life.  Prevention and treatment: Healthy eating, regular physical activity, maintain healthy body weight, monitor blood glucose and attend regular"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"tes later in life.  Prevention and treatment: Healthy eating, regular physical activity, maintain healthy body weight, monitor blood glucose and attend regular health check-ups. Treatment include lifestyle changes, oral medicines and/or insulin, depending on the type and individual needs.  Prevent and reduce the impact of Type 2 diabetes through healthy lifestyles and early management. Hypertension (High blood pressure-BP)  A chronic condition in whichblood pressure in the arteries remains persistently elevated, increasing the risk of heart disease, stroke and kidney damage.  Risk factors: High-salt intake & unhealthy diet. Physical inactivity. Overweight and obesity. Tobacco use. Harmful alcohol consumption. Family history. Prolonged exposure to air pollution.  Prevention and treatment: Reduce salt intake. Eat a healthy diet. Maintain a healthy body weight. Regular physical activity. Avoid tobacco. Limit harmful alcohol consumption. Regular BP checks. Take prescribed medication appropriately. Limit exposure to air pollution.  Hypertension can be silent, but regular BP checks & appropriate treatment help prevent heart attacks, strokes & kidney damage. Obesity  A chronic"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"pollution.  Hypertension can be silent, but regular BP checks & appropriate treatment help prevent heart attacks, strokes & kidney damage. Obesity  A chronic condition characterized by excessive body fat that can negatively affect health. It’s associated with an increased risk of type 2 diabetes, hypertension, cardiovascular diseases and certain cancers.  In adults, body mass index (BMI) over 25 is considered overweight, and over 30 is obese.  Risk factors: Unhealthy diet; particularly excessive intake of energy- dense foods and high consumption of sugary foods and drinks. Physical inactivity, Sedentary lifestyles, Genetic predisposition, Environmental factors. Limited access to healthy foods (socioeconomic factors).  Obesity develops throughcomplex interactionsamong diet, physical activity, genetics, environment, social conditions and other factors rather than simply being a consequence of individual choices. Obesity  Prevention and management : Healthy balanced diets. Regular physical activity. Reduce sedentary behaviour. Monitor body weight and seek professional support when needed. Healthcare professionals can provide nutrition counselling, assess health risks and"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ry behaviour. Monitor body weight and seek professional support when needed. Healthcare professionals can provide nutrition counselling, assess health risks and recommend appropriate treatment.  Obesity is a complex, multifactorial health condition. Prevention and management require healthy behaviours, access to healthcare and supportive environments that make food choices and physical activity more accessible. Cancer  A broad group of diseases characterized by uncontrolled growth & multiplication of abnormal cells. These cells may invade surrounding tissues & spread to other parts of the body. Cancer can develop in almost any part of the body, and different cancers have different causes, risk factors and treatment approaches.  Cancer is the 2 nd leading cause of death worldwide, after cardiovascular diseases. Most common cancers are lung, breast, colorectal, prostate & stomach cancers.  Risk factors: Tobacco use. Harmful alcohol consumption. Unhealthy diets. Physical inactivity. Overweight & obesity. Exposure to cancer- causing infections (e.g., HPV & hepatitis). Radiation & environmental pollutants/carcinogens. Genetic predisposition & increasing age. Many cancers can be"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ncer- causing infections (e.g., HPV & hepatitis). Radiation & environmental pollutants/carcinogens. Genetic predisposition & increasing age. Many cancers can be cured if detected early & treated effectively  Prevention and treatment: Avoid tobacco. Limit alcohol. Healthy diets. Healthy body weight. Regular physical activity. Vaccinations against cancer-causing infections (HPV & hepatitis B).  Reduce exposure to ultraviolet radiation exposure. Avoid urban air pollution & indoor smoke from household use of solid fuels.  Participate in appropriate cancer screening. Seek medical attention for persistent or unusual symptoms. Early diagnosis & timely treatment.  Screening and early diagnosis promotes early detection.  Treatment depends on the type & stage of cancer. It can include surgery, chemotherapy, radiotherapy, or combinations of chemotherapy, hormonal treatments & targeted biological therapies.  Palliative care focuses on improving quality of life and relieving symptoms for patients who have cancer (esp. advanced stages).  Now that your understand some of the major NCDs and their risk factors, what can you do to prevent and manage them?  As an individual: Adopt healthy"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"stages).  Now that your understand some of the major NCDs and their risk factors, what can you do to prevent and manage them?  As an individual: Adopt healthy eating habits. Engage in regular physical activity. Avoid tobacco use. Limit harmful alcohol consumption. Maintain a healthy body weight. Participate in recommended health screening. Follow prescribed treatment. Seek medical attention when concerning symptoms occur.  An important way to control NCDs is tofocus on reducing the modifiable risk factorsassociated with these diseases.  Invest in better management of NCDs: Regular screening. Early detection. Treating these diseases. Access to palliative care for people in need. Reducing the burden of NCDs is a shared responsibility of individuals, health professionals & communities. Take control of your well-being today! Get regular check-ups:  Detect problems early  Start treatment on time  Prevent complications  Stay healthy and productive Lecture 4: Mental Health Next"},{"u":"notes/03_noncommunicable-diseases.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Noncommunicable Diseases","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"","i":"04-mental-health","x":"04 · Mental Health Source: TOPIC 4 _MENTAL HEALTH.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes Topic 4: MENTAL HEALTH According to the World Health Organization (WHO) “Mental health is a state of mental well-being that enables people to cope with the stresses of life, realize their abilities, learn well and work well, and contribute to their community.” The WHO states that mental health is “more than the absence of mental disorders.” Peak mental health is about managing active conditions and maintaining wellness and happiness. The organization also emphasizes that preserving and restoring mental health is important at individual, community, and societal levels. Factors contributing to mental health issues Mental health conditions are complex and usually caused by a combination of biological, psychological, and social/lifestyle factors. No single cause explains all mental health issues — instead, different risk factors interact and increase the likelihood of developing a condition. The main contributing factors include: Biological Factors Factor How It Contributes Genetics / Family history Mental health conditions can run in families (e.g. depression, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia). Brain chemistry Imbalances in neurotransmitters like serotonin, dopamine, or"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"conditions can run in families (e.g. depression, bipolar disorder, schizophrenia). Brain chemistry Imbalances in neurotransmitters like serotonin, dopamine, or norepinephrine can affect mood and behavior. Hormonal changes Hormonal imbalances (e.g. thyroid issues, postpartum changes) can impact mental health. Prenatal exposure Exposure to drugs, alcohol, infections, or stress during pregnancy can affect brain development. Physical illness / injury Chronic conditions or brain injuries (like concussions or tumors) can contribute to mental health problems. Substance use Alcohol and drugs can trigger or worsen mental health conditions, especially anxiety, psychosis, or mood disorders. Psychological Factors Factor How It Contributes Childhood trauma or abuse Early exposure to trauma (emotional, physical, or sexual) increases risk of many disorders. Neglect or attachment issues Lack of emotional support or insecure attachment in childhood affects emotional development. Low self-esteem Chronic self-criticism, shame, or feelings of worthlessness are linked to depression and anxiety. Negative thinking patterns Rigid or distorted thinking can fuel anxiety, depression, and other issues"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ngs of worthlessness are linked to depression and anxiety. Negative thinking patterns Rigid or distorted thinking can fuel anxiety, depression, and other issues (e.g., catastrophizing). Coping skills Poor ability to manage stress, frustration, or emotions increases vulnerability to mental health issues. Social and Environmental Factors Factor How It Contributes Stressful life events Loss of a loved one, divorce, unemployment, or major illness can trigger mental health conditions. Poverty or financial stress Chronic financial insecurity is linked to higher rates of anxiety and depression. Social isolation Lack of supportive relationships increases risk of depression and emotional distress. Discrimination or stigma Racism, sexism, homophobia, or ableism can create chronic stress and lower self-worth. Unstable or unsafe environment Living in violent, chaotic, or unstable environments can contribute to mental illness. Peer pressure or bullying Especially in youth, these can lead to anxiety, self-harm, or suicidal thoughts. Lifestyle Factors Factor How It Contributes Lack of sleep Chronic sleep deprivation is strongly linked to depression, anxiety, and mood swings. Poor diet"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ughts. Lifestyle Factors Factor How It Contributes Lack of sleep Chronic sleep deprivation is strongly linked to depression, anxiety, and mood swings. Poor diet Nutritional deficiencies (e.g., B vitamins, omega-3) can affect brain function. Lack of physical activity Sedentary lifestyle increases risk of depression and cognitive decline. Substance misuse Recreational or prescription drug misuse can worsen or cause mental health symptoms. Technology overuse Excessive social media or screen time is linked to anxiety, low mood, and attention issues. Major Life Transitions or Developmental Factors Puberty, aging, and menopause can bring psychological challenges. Leaving home, starting a new job, or retiring can affect identity and stability. Becoming a parent or caregiver may increase stress and emotional strain. Risk Increases When Factors Combine: Often, mental health conditions arise when multiple risk factors overlap. For example: A person with a genetic predisposition to depression who also experiences childhood trauma, and is now under chronic financial stress, has a significantly higher risk of developing a depressive disorder. ✅ Protective Factors (That Help Prevent Mental"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":", and is now under chronic financial stress, has a significantly higher risk of developing a depressive disorder. ✅ Protective Factors (That Help Prevent Mental Illness) Strong family and social support Healthy coping skills Stable income and housing Access to mental health care Good sleep, diet, and exercise Positive self-esteem and identity Safe and supportive community environment Types of mental health disorders Healthcare professionals group mental health disorders according to the features they have in common. They include: anxiety disorders mood disorders schizophrenia disorders ANXIETY DISORDERS Anxiety disorders are the most common mental health disorders. People with these conditions have severe fear or anxiety related to certain objects or situations. Most people with an anxiety disorder try to avoid exposure to whatever triggers their anxiety. Below are some examples of anxiety disorders. i). Generalized anxiety disorder Generalized anxiety disorder (GAD) involves excessive worry or fear that disrupts everyday living. People may also experience physical symptoms, including: restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, tense muscles and interrupted sleep Anxiety symptoms"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"living. People may also experience physical symptoms, including: restlessness, fatigue, poor concentration, tense muscles and interrupted sleep Anxiety symptoms do not always have a specific trigger in people with GAD. They may experience excessive anxiety when encountering everyday situations that do not pose a direct danger, such as chores or appointments. A person with GAD may sometimes feel anxiety with no trigger at all. ii). Panic disorder People with panic disorder experience regular panic attacks involving sudden, overwhelming terror or a sense of imminent disaster and death. iii). Phobias There are many types of phobias, and what may seem typical to one person might be a severe problem that dominates daily life for another. Different types include: Simple phobias: These may involve a disproportionate fear of specific objects, scenarios, or animals. A fear of spiders is a typical example. Social phobia: Sometimes known as social anxiety, this is a fear of being watched or judged by others. People with social phobia often restrict their exposure to social environments. Agoraphobia: The term “agoraphobia” refers to a fear of situations where getting away may be difficult,"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ia often restrict their exposure to social environments. Agoraphobia: The term “agoraphobia” refers to a fear of situations where getting away may be difficult, such as being in an elevator or a moving train. People sometimes misunderstand this phobia as a fear of being outside. iv). Obsessive-compulsive disorder People with obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) may experience constant, stressful thoughts and an urge to perform repetitive acts, such as handwashing. v). Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) PTSD can occur after a person experiences or witnesses a stressful or traumatic event. During this type of event, the person thinks their life or other people’s lives are in danger. They may feel afraid or that they have no control over what is happening. These sensations of trauma and fear may then contribute to PTSD. What are traumatic events? Examples of events that can trigger PTSD include accidents, wars, crimes, fires, the death of a loved one, abuse, or any event that causes shock and fear. Thoughts and memories may recur even though the danger has passed. MOOD DISORDERS These may also be referred to as affective disorders or depressive disorders. People with these"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ries may recur even though the danger has passed. MOOD DISORDERS These may also be referred to as affective disorders or depressive disorders. People with these conditions have significant mood changes, generally involving either mania, a period of high energy and joy, or depression. Examples of mood disorders include: Major depression: An individual with major depression experiences a constant low mood and loses interest in activities and events that they previously enjoyed (anhedonia). They may feel prolonged periods of sadness or extreme sadness. Bipolar disorder: A person with bipolar disorder experiences unusual changes in their mood, energy levels, levels of activity, and ability to continue with daily life. Periods of high mood are known as manic phases, while depressive phases bring on low mood. Seasonal affective disorder (SAD): Reduced daylight during the fall, winter, and early spring months triggers SAD, a type of major depression. It is most common in countries far from the equator. Read more about the different types of bipolar disorder. SCHIZOPHRENIA DISORDERS The term “schizophrenia” often refers to a spectrum of disorders characterized by features of psychosis and"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"t types of bipolar disorder. SCHIZOPHRENIA DISORDERS The term “schizophrenia” often refers to a spectrum of disorders characterized by features of psychosis and other severe symptoms. These are highly complex conditions. Signs of schizophrenia typically develop between the ages of 16 and 30. A person will have thoughts that appear fragmented, and may also find it hard to process information. Schizophrenia has negative and positive symptoms. Positive symptoms include delusions, thought disorders, and hallucinations, while withdrawal, lack of motivation, and a flat or inappropriate mood are examples of negative symptoms. Early signs of mental illness No physical test or scan reliably indicates whether a person has developed a mental health condition. However, people should look out for the following as possible signs: withdrawing from friends, family, and colleagues avoiding activities, they would usually enjoy sleeping too much or too little eating too much or too little feeling hopeless having consistently low energy using mood-altering substances, including alcohol and nicotine, more frequently displaying negative emotions being confused being unable to complete daily tasks, such"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"mood-altering substances, including alcohol and nicotine, more frequently displaying negative emotions being confused being unable to complete daily tasks, such as getting to work or cooking a meal having persistent thoughts or memories that reappear regularly thinking of causing physical harm to themselves or others hearing voices experiencing delusions Diagnosis Diagnosing a mental health disorder requires a multistep process. A doctor may begin by looking at a person’s medical history and performing a thorough physical exam to rule out a physical condition that may be causing the symptoms. They may also order laboratory tests, such as imaging exams and blood tests, to screen for possible underlying causes. The healthcare professional will also do a psychological evaluation. This includes asking about a person’s symptoms and experiences and how these affect the individual’s life. The doctor may ask a person to complete mental health questionnaires to help understand their thoughts, feelings, and behavior patterns., Treatment/management of mental health Various methods are available to help manage mental health conditions. Treatment is highly individual, and what works for one"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"atment/management of mental health Various methods are available to help manage mental health conditions. Treatment is highly individual, and what works for one person may not work for another. Some strategies or treatments are more successful in combination with others. A person with a mental health disorder may choose different options at various stages in their life. It is important for an individual to work closely with a doctor who can help them identify their needs and provide suitable treatment. Below are some treatment options for people with mental health conditions. (1. Psychotherapy Medication 3. Self-help Psychotherapy, or talking therapies The following types of therapy take a psychological approach to treating mental health: i). Cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT) Is a therapy technique that aims to help people find new ways to behave by changing their thought patterns. Therapists may use strategies such as role-playing and assigning homework. How does it work? Some forms of psychotherapy focus on looking into the past to understand current feelings. Instead, CBT focuses on present thoughts and beliefs. It emphasizes the need to identify, challenge, and change how a"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"o the past to understand current feelings. Instead, CBT focuses on present thoughts and beliefs. It emphasizes the need to identify, challenge, and change how a person views a situation. Although counselors typically personalize sessions to an individual, CBT steps may include: an individual assessment establishing goals and areas to work on early in the process regularly checking the person’s mood and any life updates collaboratively setting agendas for each session reviewing any homework punctuating discussions with feedback and summaries assigning further homework at the end of sessions Step-by-Step CBT Plan for Panic Attacks Step 1: Understand Your Panic Goal: Learn what panic attacks are and why they happen. Panic is a false alarm — your body’s fight-or-flight system overreacts. Symptoms (like rapid heartbeat, dizziness, breathlessness) are not harmful, just uncomfortable. ✅ Action: Read about the fight-or-flight response. Write down what triggers your panic and what you fear will happen. Step 2: Identify and Challenge Your Thoughts Goal: Catch and question catastrophic thinking that fuels panic. ✅ Action: Use this 5-column Thought Record Worksheet: Situation Physical"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"hallenge Your Thoughts Goal: Catch and question catastrophic thinking that fuels panic. ✅ Action: Use this 5-column Thought Record Worksheet: Situation Physical Symptoms Automatic Thought Evidence For / Against Balanced Thought Where were you? What happened? What did you feel physically? What went through your mind? Is that 100% true? What else could be true? A calmer, realistic thought. Step 3: Interoceptive Exposure (Face the Feelings) Goal: Desensitize yourself to the physical sensations of panic. ✅ Action: Practice exercises that simulate symptoms: Exercise Simulated Symptom Instructions Spin in a chair Dizziness Spin for 30 seconds Breathe through a straw Breathlessness For 1 minute Run in place Rapid heartbeat For 1–2 minutes Tense muscles Tight chest Hold, then release ➡️ Sit with the sensations until they fade. Say: “I can handle this. It's just my body reacting.” Step 4: In Vivo Exposure (Face Your Fears) Goal: Stop avoiding feared situations. ✅ Action: Make a fear hierarchy (from least to most feared situations). Example: Standing in a short line at a store Taking a short bus ride Attending a small event Going to a crowded mall ➡️ Gradually face each step until anxiety"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":". Example: Standing in a short line at a store Taking a short bus ride Attending a small event Going to a crowded mall ➡️ Gradually face each step until anxiety drops. Don’t escape early. Step 5: Breathing & Grounding Techniques Goal: Calm your body and stay grounded in the present. ✅ Action: Practice this 4-7-8 breathing technique daily: Inhale through your nose for 4 seconds Hold your breath for 7 seconds Exhale slowly through your mouth for 8 seconds Try also: 5-4-3-2-1 Grounding 5 things you see 4 things you can touch 3 things you hear 2 things you smell 1 thing you taste Step 6: Prevent Relapse Goal: Stay resilient and manage future flare-ups. ✅ Action: Track stress levels and sleep Continue small exposures regularly Keep using CBT tools Create a relapse plan: What will you do if symptoms return? 📚 Self-Help Book: “Panic Attacks Workbook” by David Carbonell “When Panic Attacks” by Dr. David Burns ii). Exposure therapy Exposure therapy is a psychological treatment that helps individuals confront and reduce fear, anxiety, or distress related to specific stimuli or situations. It's primarily used to treat various anxiety disorders, but also has applications in PTSD, OCD, and"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"iety, or distress related to specific stimuli or situations. It's primarily used to treat various anxiety disorders, but also has applications in PTSD, OCD, and other mental health conditions. It is a type of cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) that involves: Gradually exposing a person to the feared object, thought, or situation. Helping them face their fears rather than avoid them. Reducing avoidance behaviors and distorted thinking that reinforce anxiety. Conditions Is It Used For include: Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD) Exposure to trauma-related memories, images, or situations in a safe and controlled way. Often includes Prolonged Exposure (PE) therapy. Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD) Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP): Patients are exposed to obsessive thoughts but prevented from performing compulsions. Phobias For example, fear of flying, spiders, or heights. Panic Disorder Exposure to physical sensations associated with panic (like racing heart) to reduce fear of those sensations. Social Anxiety Disorder Exposure to feared social situations like public speaking or initiating conversation. Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) Less commonly used, but sometimes"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"order Exposure to feared social situations like public speaking or initiating conversation. Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) Less commonly used, but sometimes integrated with CBT techniques. Types of Exposure Type Description In Vivo Exposure Real-life exposure to feared situations (e.g., touching a doorknob if you have germ phobia). Imaginal Exposure Vividly imagining the feared situation or memory (commonly used in PTSD). Interoceptive Exposure Inducing physical sensations (e.g., dizziness, rapid heartbeat) to reduce fear of those sensations (used in panic disorder). Virtual Reality Exposure Using VR to simulate environments (e.g., for fear of flying or combat-related PTSD). How It Works (The Psychology) Based on the principles of habituation, extinction learning, and cognitive restructuring. Repeated exposure helps reduce anxiety over time because the brain learns the feared outcome doesn’t happen or is manageable. Also helps people develop coping skills and tolerate uncertainty iii).Dialectical behavior therapy (DBT) is a type of cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) that was originally developed by Dr. Marsha Linehan in the late 1980s to treat individuals with borderline"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"(DBT) is a type of cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT) that was originally developed by Dr. Marsha Linehan in the late 1980s to treat individuals with borderline personality disorder (BPD) and chronic suicidality. Since then, it's been adapted for a wide range of mental health issues DBT combines: Cognitive-behavioral techniques (to change unhelpful thinking and behaviors) Mindfulness practices (from Zen Buddhism) A dialectical approach: balancing acceptance of yourself as you are, with commitment to change Core Principle is the “Dialectic” The word dialectical refers to the idea of holding two seemingly opposite things as true at the same time — for example: “I am doing the best I can” and “I need to try harder to change.” This balance between acceptance and change is the foundation of DBT. The four core principles of DBT Mindfulness Teaches you to observe, describe, and participate in the present moment. Helps you become more aware of thoughts and feelings without being overwhelmed by them. Distress Tolerance Teaches you how to cope with crises and emotional pain without making the situation worse. Includes skills like: Self-soothing, Distraction, Radical acceptance and Improving"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ow to cope with crises and emotional pain without making the situation worse. Includes skills like: Self-soothing, Distraction, Radical acceptance and Improving the moment Emotion Regulation Helps you understand and manage intense emotions. Skills include: Identifying and labeling emotions Reducing emotional vulnerability Changing unwanted emotional responses Interpersonal Effectiveness Focuses on how to: Ask for what you need Set healthy boundaries Maintain relationships Build self-respect in interactions Psychiatrists, psychologists, psychotherapists, and some primary care physicians carry out these treatments. Therapy may help individuals to understand the cause of their mental health condition. Working with a healthcare professional may help people manage their day-to-day living and reduce any risk of isolation and self-harm. Medication Some people take prescribed medications, such as antidepressants, antipsychotics, and drugs for anxiety. Although these medications cannot cure mental health disorders, some can help improve symptoms. They may also help a person to manage their social interactions and routines. Some medications boost the body’s absorption of feel-good"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"n help improve symptoms. They may also help a person to manage their social interactions and routines. Some medications boost the body’s absorption of feel-good chemicals, such as serotonin, from the brain. Other drugs either boost the levels of these chemicals or help prevent their degradation or destruction. Self-help A person coping with a mental health condition may decide to make certain lifestyle changes to help them manage their well-being. Changes may include: reducing alcohol intake, if applicable trying to improve sleep quality eating a balanced, nutritious diet taking time away from work/intense study, if this is possible practicing relaxation techniques, such as deep breathing, meditation, and mindfulness A support network, whether through self-help groups or close friends and family, can be helpful to a person’s well-being. Outlook While mental health disorders are common, they vary in severity. Many people can manage their symptoms and lead full lives with treatment and access to support. For others, recovery may include learning new ways to help manage their lives. The prevalence of mental health disorders tends to peak in people ages 18–25 but drops significantly"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"y may include learning new ways to help manage their lives. The prevalence of mental health disorders tends to peak in people ages 18–25 but drops significantly among people ages 50 and over. Having a mental health condition, especially depression, is associated with severe chronic health conditions such as diabetes, stroke, cardiovascular disease, and Alzheimer’s disease. Steps to help maintain mental well-being The following steps can help a person manage their mental health by reducing their risk of illness, increasing energy levels, and controlling stress: Regular exercise: Exercising for 45 minutes three to five times a week can help improve a person’s mental health. Eat a balanced diet and stay hydrated: This can help a person maintain a constant supply of energy throughout the day. Aim for good-quality sleep: A 2021 review found that significant improvements in sleep quality led to greater improvements in mental health. Perform relaxing activities: Breathing exercises, meditation, wellness apps, and journaling can help reduce stress and improve overall health and well-being. Practice mindfulness and gratitude: A person can identify and note things they are grateful for at"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"help reduce stress and improve overall health and well-being. Practice mindfulness and gratitude: A person can identify and note things they are grateful for at the end of each day. Look for positive social interactions: Connecting with people and maintaining meaningful relationships can help reduce stress and be a source of support and practical help in times of need. Role of regular Exercise in maintaining Mental Well-Being Regular physical exercise is one of the most powerful natural tools for maintaining and improving mental well-being. It's not just about getting physically fit — it's also about creating chemical, emotional, and psychological benefits that protect your mental health. Boosts Mood and Reduces Depression Exercise increases \"feel-good\" brain chemicals like: Endorphins (natural painkillers and mood elevators) Serotonin (linked to mood regulation and happiness) Dopamine (involved in motivation and pleasure) Regular physical activity is shown to be as effective as antidepressants in some cases of mild to moderate depression. Reduces Anxiety and Stress Exercise reduces levels of stress hormones like cortisol. It improves your ability to manage anxiety through:"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"to moderate depression. Reduces Anxiety and Stress Exercise reduces levels of stress hormones like cortisol. It improves your ability to manage anxiety through: Distraction from worries Relaxation of muscle tension Building resilience through facing physical challenges Improves Sleep Quality Physical activity helps you fall asleep faster and sleep more deeply, which is vital for emotional regulation and mental clarity. Better sleep = better mood, focus, and stress tolerance. Enhances Brain Function Exercise increases blood flow to the brain, improving: Memory Concentration Executive function (planning, organizing, decision-making) It also stimulates neurogenesis (growth of new brain cells), especially in the hippocampus, a region linked to emotion and memory. Boosts Self-Esteem and Confidence Achieving fitness goals or simply being active can make you feel more capable and in control. Improves body image and confidence, even without major physical changes. Increases Social Connection Group exercise (like team sports, gym classes, or walking groups) provides social interaction, which is crucial for mental health. Reduces loneliness and can lead to supportive relationships. Supports"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"asses, or walking groups) provides social interaction, which is crucial for mental health. Reduces loneliness and can lead to supportive relationships. Supports Routine and Structure Having a regular exercise routine helps create predictability, which is comforting during stressful or uncertain times. Helps anchor your day, giving it a sense of rhythm and purpose. Summary on Benefits of Regular Exercise on Mental Health Benefit Mental Impact Increases endorphins Better mood, energy, positivity Lowers cortisol Reduced stress and anxiety Improves sleep Better focus, mood, and energy levels Builds self-efficacy Increases confidence and motivation Enhances neuroplasticity Better memory and thinking skills Encourages socialization Less loneliness and better relationships Types of Exercise Different people benefit from different forms of exercise. Examples: Walking / Running – Reduces depression and clears the mind. Yoga / Pilates – Combines movement with mindfulness and breathing. Strength Training – Boosts confidence and combats anxiety. Team Sports / Dance – Promotes social connection and joy. Additional reading What is post-traumatic stress disorder or PTSD? PTSD is a mental health"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ts anxiety. Team Sports / Dance – Promotes social connection and joy. Additional reading What is post-traumatic stress disorder or PTSD? PTSD is a mental health condition that can develop following a traumatic event. People with PTSD have intense, distressing thoughts related to the event and may have flashbacks and nightmares. Fear and shock are natural responses to trauma, and most people recover from these with time. If anxiety and other negative feelings continue, doctors may diagnose PTSD. PTSD can disrupt a person’s life for years, but treatment can help them recover. What are traumatic events? Examples of events that can trigger PTSD include accidents, wars, crimes, fires, the death of a loved one, abuse, or any event that causes shock and fear. Thoughts and memories may recur even though the danger has passed. Types of PTSD Mental health professionals may diagnose various subtypes of PTSD. These subtypes may have different underlying causes and symptoms. Subtypes include: Complex PTSD: This may develop following repeated trauma, particularly in childhood. Dissociative PTSD (D-PTSD): People with D-PTSD may experience dissociative symptoms, such as feeling that they or the"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"epeated trauma, particularly in childhood. Dissociative PTSD (D-PTSD): People with D-PTSD may experience dissociative symptoms, such as feeling that they or the world around them are not real. Birth trauma: This can occur following a traumatic childbirth experience. Delayed expression PTSD: People with this subtype experience symptoms 6 months or more after a traumatic event. PTSD for children 6 years and younger: This subtype recognizes the specific signs of PTSD that young children may express. Only D-PTSD and the preschool subtype are included in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, 5th edition (DSM-5). Psychiatrists use the DSM-5 to help diagnose mental health conditions. Symptoms of PTSD in adults Symptoms usually start within 3 months of the traumatic event, but they can begin later. For a person to receive a diagnosis of PTSD, they must meet criteria set out in the DSM-5. According to these guidelines, the person must: experience exposure to death or a personal death threat, a serious injury or sexual violence whether directly, as a witness, by it happening to a loved one, or during professional duties experience the following for more than 1 month:"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"or sexual violence whether directly, as a witness, by it happening to a loved one, or during professional duties experience the following for more than 1 month: one or more intrusion symptoms one or more avoidance symptoms two or more symptoms that affect mood and thinking two or more arousal and reactivity symptoms that began after the trauma Below are examples of these types of symptoms: Intrusion symptoms: include: nightmares flashbacks and a sensation that the event is happening again fearful thoughts Avoidance symptoms: include: refusing to discuss the event avoiding situations that remind the person of the event Symptoms that affect mood and thinking These include: inability to remember some aspects of the event feelings of guilt and blame feeling detached and estranged from others and emotionally and mentally numbed having reduced interest in life difficulty concentrating mental health problems, such as depression, phobias, and anxiety Arousal and reactivity symptoms: include: difficulty sleeping irritability and angry outbursts hypersensitivity to possible dangers feeling tense and anxious In addition, these symptoms must cause a person distress or difficulty coping with"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ngry outbursts hypersensitivity to possible dangers feeling tense and anxious In addition, these symptoms must cause a person distress or difficulty coping with work or relationships. The symptoms must not be due to the use of medications or other substances or another health condition. Physical symptoms of PTSD People may also have the following physical symptoms of PTSD, but the DSM-5 criteria do not include these: physical effects, including sweating, shaking, headaches, stomach problems, dizziness, aches and pains, and chest pain a weakened immune system, which can lead to more frequent infections sleep disturbances that can result in tiredness and other problems A person may experience long-term behavioral changes that contribute to problems at work and a breakdown in their relationships. They may start to seek numbing behaviors, such as misusing alcohol, drugs, or medications. In severe cases, PTSD may cause anger outbursts that make it difficult for the person to control. This would create a barrier in maintaining a relationship in which both parties feel safe. In instances where trauma is due to sexual violence, it might be particularly difficult to establish healthy"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ing a relationship in which both parties feel safe. In instances where trauma is due to sexual violence, it might be particularly difficult to establish healthy boundaries and expectations in regards to intimacy. —Andy Watkins-McCall, MD, MPH Symptoms of PTSD in children and teens In children, symptoms of PTSD may include: bed wetting after learning to use the bathroom an inability to speak acting out the event in their play being clingy with an adult Children may not have flashbacks, but be able to remember the traumatic event clearly. However, they may remember it in a different order or feel that there was a sign that it was going to happen. Children may also act out or express their trauma through their play, drawings, and stories. They may have nightmares, act irritable, and find it hard to go to school, study, or spend time with friends. Starting from 8 years of age, children’s reactions are generally similar to adults. Between the ages of 12 and 18 years, the child may show disruptive, disrespectful, impulsive, or aggressive behavior. They may feel guilty for not acting differently during the event, or they may consider revenge. Children who have experienced sexual abuse"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"aggressive behavior. They may feel guilty for not acting differently during the event, or they may consider revenge. Children who have experienced sexual abuse are more likely to: feel fear, sadness, anxiety, and isolation have low self-esteem behave in an aggressive manner display unusual sexual behavior hurt themselves misuse drugs or alcohol Causes and Risk Factors of PTSD PTSD can develop after any traumatic event. Any situation that triggers fear, shock, horror, or helplessness can lead to PTSD. Examples include: military service, natural disasters, serious accidents, terrorist attacks, loss of a loved one, whether or not this involved violence, rape or other types of abuse, personal assault, being a victim of crime and receiving a life-threatening diagnosis etc. It is unclear why some people develop PTSD while others do not. However, the following risk factors may increase the chances of a person experiencing symptoms: having additional problems after an event, for example, losing a loved one and losing a job lacking social support after an event having a history of mental health problems or substance misuse experience of abuse, for example, during childhood getting a"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"acking social support after an event having a history of mental health problems or substance misuse experience of abuse, for example, during childhood getting a physical injury, possibly as a result of the event What reduces the risk? Scientists are looking at resilience factors that may help people recover from or avoid PTSD more effectively. These include: seeking out and receiving support from others having or developing positive coping strategies being able to feel good about their actions when facing difficulty percussions on unemployment in Kenya, creating a multifaceted impact on the nation's workforce.10 These downturns often trigger job losses across diverse sectors as businesses downsize, freeze hiring, or shut down due to decreased consumer spending and financial instability.11 The reduction in investment, both domestic and foreign, during recessions limits new job creation, with companies scaling back expansion plans and projects. The informal sector, integral to Kenya's economy, is particularly vulnerable, leading to income instability for many workers. Disruptions in global supply chains during recess"},{"u":"notes/04_mental-health.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Mental Health","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"","i":"05-drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya","x":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya Source: TOPIC 5 DRUG AND SUBSTANCE ABUSE IN KENYA.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes --- slide 36 --- Educational Consequences Among students and young people, substance misuse can interfere with education and personal development. Possible consequences include: Poor concentration and memory Declining academic performance Absenteeism and lateness Loss of interest in studies Disciplinary problems Dropping out of school or college Reduced participation in extracurricular activities Difficulty completing assignments and examinations Early substance use can therefore interfere with educational opportunities and future career development. --- slide 36 --- Social and Family Consequences Addiction rarely affects only the individual. Families and social relationships can also suffer. Frequent arguments and conflict within the family Loss of trust Social isolation Separation or divorce Neglect of children or other dependants Domestic violence in some cases Loss of friendships and social support Financial dependence on family members Emotional stress among parents, partners, siblings, and children Family members may experience fear, shame, anxiety, anger, helplessness, or financial hardship. --- slide 36 --- Legal and Criminal Consequences Substance misuse can"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"y members may experience fear, shame, anxiety, anger, helplessness, or financial hardship. --- slide 36 --- Legal and Criminal Consequences Substance misuse can increase exposure to legal problems, although substance use itself is treated differently under the laws of different countries. Possible consequences include: Arrest or prosecution for possession or trafficking where prohibited Driving under the influence Disorderly or violent behavior Theft or other crimes committed to obtain money for substances Legal expenses and fines Imprisonment A criminal record that may affect employment and education It is important to distinguish between substance use , which is a health issue, and criminal behavior associated with or surrounding substance use. --- slide 36 --- Occupational and Economic Consequences Addiction can affect an individual's ability to obtain and maintain employment. Consequences may include: Reduced productivity Absenteeism Accidents and injuries at work Poor relationships with colleagues or supervisors Job loss Difficulty finding future employment Reduced income Spending large amounts of money on substances Debt and financial instability Dependence on relatives or"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"loss Difficulty finding future employment Reduced income Spending large amounts of money on substances Debt and financial instability Dependence on relatives or social services The economic consequences may extend to families when household income is diverted toward substance use rather than food, housing, education, or healthcare. --- slide 36 --- Cognitive and Behavioral Consequences Substance abuse can interfere with judgment, concentration, memory, decision-making, and self-control. A person may: Make impulsive or risky decisions. Have difficulty concentrating or remembering information. Become increasingly preoccupied with obtaining and using the substance. Neglect responsibilities and personal hygiene. Display aggression, irritability, or unpredictable behavior. Engage in dangerous activities, including driving while impaired. Experience withdrawal symptoms when they stop or reduce use. Over time, obtaining and using the substance may become a central priority, replacing previously important activities and relationships. --- slide 36 --- Previous substance use The use of one substance can sometimes increase exposure to other substances. For example, someone who regularly"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"nships. --- slide 36 --- Previous substance use The use of one substance can sometimes increase exposure to other substances. For example, someone who regularly drinks alcohol or uses tobacco may encounter social environments where other drugs are available. This is sometimes discussed as polysubstance use , meaning the use of multiple substances, either at the same time or during the same period. --- slide 36 --- Cultural and community factors Attitudes toward alcohol and other substances differ between communities. Risk can increase where: Heavy drinking is socially accepted. Drug use is common within a community. There are few prevention programs. Treatment services are difficult to access. Substance use is associated with particular social activities. Conversely, strong community support and clear social norms against harmful substance use can provide protection. --- slide 36 --- Psychological and Mental Health Consequences Addiction can substantially affect emotional and psychological well-being. Anxiety, irritability, and agitation Depression and emotional instability Panic attacks Paranoia and suspiciousness Hallucinations or psychotic symptoms with some substances Sleep"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ty, and agitation Depression and emotional instability Panic attacks Paranoia and suspiciousness Hallucinations or psychotic symptoms with some substances Sleep disturbances Reduced ability to cope with stress Dependence on substances to feel normal or function Worsening of pre-existing mental health problems There can also be a harmful cycle in which substance use temporarily relieves distress but ultimately worsens mental health, leading to further substance use. --- slide 36 --- Broadly consequences of substance abuse/addiction can be explained in following headings: 1. Physical/Health Consequences Substance abuse can damage almost every major organ system, depending on the substance, dose, duration, and route of use. Brain and nervous system: impaired coordination, memory problems, seizures, changes in brain function, and neurological damage. Heart and blood vessels: abnormal heart rhythms, high or low blood pressure, heart attack, stroke, and other cardiovascular problems. Liver: particularly with alcohol and some drugs, prolonged use can cause fatty liver, hepatitis, cirrhosis, and liver failure. Lungs: smoking or inhaling substances can cause chronic respiratory problems,"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ugs, prolonged use can cause fatty liver, hepatitis, cirrhosis, and liver failure. Lungs: smoking or inhaling substances can cause chronic respiratory problems, lung infections, and other pulmonary damage. Kidneys: some substances can contribute to kidney injury or failure. Immune system: substance misuse may weaken health and increase susceptibility to infections. Nutrition: addiction may lead to poor appetite, inadequate nutrition, weight loss, or vitamin deficiencies. Overdose: excessive consumption can cause respiratory depression, unconsciousness, coma, permanent organ damage, or death. --- slide 36 --- ( b) Community and family involvement Local leaders and elders monitoring brewing and sale of illegal substances. Churches and mosques offering counseling, mentorship, and rehabilitation. • Families providing early guidance, monitoring, and positive role modeling. (c) Rehabilitation and recovery Rehab centers offering detox, counseling, and reintegration programs. Peer support groups like Alcoholics Anonymous (AA). --- slide 36 --- Strategies to Address Drug and Substance Abuse in Kenya (a) Government strategies NACADA campaigns to educate the public. Ban on second-generation"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"slide 36 --- Strategies to Address Drug and Substance Abuse in Kenya (a) Government strategies NACADA campaigns to educate the public. Ban on second-generation and illicit brews. Laws restricting alcohol sales near schools and regulating drinking hours. Community policing and crackdowns on drug trafficking. --- slide 36 --- Legal Framework on Drug and Substance Abuse The alarming prevalence of drug abuse in the country and among the youth in particular has continued despite the existing legislation and its enforcement by the authorities. The government made legislations to deal with drugs, to control access of drugs to certain categories of people and rightly outlaw the use of others, some of the laws in the statute books that specifically are aimed at controlling drug use. The government enacted the liquor licensing Act in 1986 among others regulate the consumption of alcohol, the law prohibits bar to sell alcohol to school going children and under aged children. In 1994 the Narcotics Drugs and psychotropic substances control Act was enacted lagging heavy penalties on drug traffickers. The National Policy on Drug Abuse in Kenya was developed on the premise that the Kenya"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"nces control Act was enacted lagging heavy penalties on drug traffickers. The National Policy on Drug Abuse in Kenya was developed on the premise that the Kenya Government had ratified three major UN conventions on narcotic drugs and psychotropic substances , namely the Single Convention on Psychotropic Substances of 1961 ; The Convention on Psychotropic Substances of 1971 and the Convention against Illicit Trafficking in Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substances of 1988. --- slide 36 --- (d) Youth empowerment Government and NGOs creating skill development, sports, and entrepreneurship programs to give youth alternatives to drugs. (e) School programs – life skills education, peer counseling, and mentorship. --- slide 36 --- Treatment, Recovery, and Relapse-Related Consequences Addiction is a treatable health condition, but recovery may involve significant challenges. Withdrawal can produce uncomfortable or, with some substances, medically dangerous symptoms. Treatment may require medical care, counseling, behavioral therapy, rehabilitation, or medication depending on the substance and individual circumstances. Relapse can occur and should generally be viewed as a signal that"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"herapy, rehabilitation, or medication depending on the substance and individual circumstances. Relapse can occur and should generally be viewed as a signal that treatment needs to be reassessed rather than simply as a moral failure. Recovery may require rebuilding relationships, employment, education, finances, and social support. --- slide 36 --- Effects on Children and Future Generations Children living with parental substance addiction may experience significant difficulties. These may include: Neglect or inadequate supervision Emotional insecurity Poor school performance Exposure to conflict or violence Financial hardship Social stigma Increased risk of developing substance-use problems themselves Substance exposure during pregnancy can also affect fetal development, with consequences varying according to the substance and pattern of exposure. --- slide 36 --- Sexual and Reproductive Health Consequences Some substances can impair judgment and increase risky sexual behavior. Possible consequences include: Unprotected sexual intercourse Multiple or high-risk sexual partners Increased exposure to sexually transmitted infections, including HIV Sexual dysfunction Reduced fertility"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"xual intercourse Multiple or high-risk sexual partners Increased exposure to sexually transmitted infections, including HIV Sexual dysfunction Reduced fertility in some circumstances Pregnancy complications Poor prenatal care Harm to a developing fetus when certain substances are used during pregnancy Intoxication can also reduce a person's ability to make informed decisions or recognize unsafe situations. --- slide 36 --- Overdose, Disability, and Death One of the most serious consequences of substance misuse is overdose . An overdose can occur when a person takes more of a substance than the body can safely tolerate, or when substances interact dangerously. Depending on the substance, overdose may result in: Severe respiratory depression Loss of consciousness Seizures Cardiac complications Brain injury Coma Permanent disability Death --- slide 36 --- Community and Societal Consequences At the broader level, widespread substance misuse can place substantial pressure on communities and public services. These consequences can include: Increased healthcare costs Increased demand for addiction treatment and rehabilitation Workplace and road accidents Crime and violence associated"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"s can include: Increased healthcare costs Increased demand for addiction treatment and rehabilitation Workplace and road accidents Crime and violence associated with some patterns of substance use Homelessness and social instability Reduced workforce productivity Increased burden on law-enforcement and judicial systems Loss of life and disability Greater strain on families and social-support systems Thus, addiction should not be viewed solely as an individual problem; it can become a significant public-health and social problem . --- slide 36 --- Family influence The family environment can strongly influence substance-use behavior. Risk may increase when: Parents or caregivers misuse alcohol or drugs. There is frequent conflict within the family. Children receive little supervision or guidance. There is neglect or abuse. Family members normalize heavy drinking or drug use. Communication between parents and children is poor. A family history of substance-use problems can also contribute through both genetic vulnerability and learned behavior . --- slide 36 --- Mental health problems Mental health conditions can increase the risk of problematic substance use. These may include:"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"y and learned behavior . --- slide 36 --- Mental health problems Mental health conditions can increase the risk of problematic substance use. These may include: Depression Anxiety Post-traumatic stress Bipolar disorder Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) Certain personality disorders Trauma-related problems Some individuals may use substances to reduce unpleasant symptoms, improve their mood, sleep, or confidence. This is sometimes called self-medication . --- slide 36 --- Availability and accessibility of drugs The easier substances are to obtain, the greater the opportunity for use. Availability can be influenced by: Illegal drug markets Alcohol outlets Tobacco availability Prescription-drug access Weak enforcement of age restrictions Drugs being readily available within a person's social network Easy access does not automatically cause addiction, but it can make initiation and continued use more likely. --- slide 36 --- Genetics and biological factors Genes can influence how vulnerable an individual is to developing a substance-use disorder. Some people may inherit characteristics that affect: How strongly they experience the rewarding effects of a drug. How their"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"eveloping a substance-use disorder. Some people may inherit characteristics that affect: How strongly they experience the rewarding effects of a drug. How their brain responds to stress. Impulse control and risk-taking. The likelihood of developing dependence. Genetics do not mean that someone is destined to become addicted. Rather, they can increase or decrease susceptibility when combined with environmental and behavioral factors. --- slide 36 --- Stress and emotional problems Some people use drugs as a way of coping with difficult emotions or stressful situations. Common sources of stress include: Financial difficulties Relationship problems Academic pressure Unemployment Family conflicts Bereavement Work-related stress Social discrimination Chronic stress A person may temporarily feel relaxed or emotionally numb after taking a substance. However, relying on drugs to manage stress can create a cycle in which the person increasingly depends on the substance. --- slide 36 --- Commonly Abused Drugs and Substances in Kenya Alcohol – the most widely abused substance, including illicit brews ( chang’aa , busaa ) and legal drinks. Tobacco – both cigarettes and smokeless forms."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Kenya Alcohol – the most widely abused substance, including illicit brews ( chang’aa , busaa ) and legal drinks. Tobacco – both cigarettes and smokeless forms. Cannabis (bhang/marijuana) – widely abused, especially among young people. Khat (miraa/ muguka ) – a legal stimulant crop but often abused, leading to addiction and health issues. Prescription drugs – misuse of painkillers (codeine, tramadol) and cough syrups. Hard drugs – heroin, cocaine, and methamphetamine, especially in coastal towns like Mombasa and urban centers like Nairobi. Inhalants – glue, petrol, and other solvents, mostly among street children. --- slide 36 --- TOPIC 5: DRUG AND SUBSTANCE ABUSE IN KENYA Dr. Susan Wairimu 11/310/2026 --- slide 36 --- Curiosity and experimentation Curiosity is an important reason why some people first try drugs. People may wonder: What does the substance feel like? Why do other people enjoy it? Will it make me feel more confident or energetic? What will happen if I try it just once? Although experimentation does not always lead to addiction, repeated use can increase the risk of developing problematic use. --- slide 36 --- Causes of Drug and Substances Abusive 1. Peer pressure"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ays lead to addiction, repeated use can increase the risk of developing problematic use. --- slide 36 --- Causes of Drug and Substances Abusive 1. Peer pressure and social influence People, particularly adolescents and young adults, may begin using drugs because their friends or social groups use them. Friends may encourage someone to experiment with alcohol, cannabis, tobacco, or other drugs. A person may use substances to feel accepted or avoid being excluded. Some social groups may portray drug use as fashionable, enjoyable, or a sign of maturity. Continued exposure to people who use drugs can make substance use seem normal. Example: A student may start smoking or drinking because classmates regularly do so and encourage them to participate. --- slide 36 --- Personality and behavioral factors Certain characteristics can increase vulnerability, although they do not directly cause addiction. Examples include: Impulsivity Sensation-seeking Difficulty controlling behavior Poor decision-making Low frustration tolerance Desire for immediate rewards Risk-taking behavior These characteristics may make experimentation or continued substance use more likely. --- slide 36 --- Easy access"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"e for immediate rewards Risk-taking behavior These characteristics may make experimentation or continued substance use more likely. --- slide 36 --- Easy access to prescription medicines Some people develop substance-use problems after using medications prescribed for legitimate medical reasons. For example, certain prescription medications can produce dependence when misused or taken for longer than medically appropriate. Risk can increase when medicines are: Taken in larger amounts than prescribed. Used more frequently than prescribed. Taken without a prescription. Shared with other people. Combined with other substances. Prescription medicines should therefore be used according to medical instructions. --- slide 36 --- Academic and workplace pressure Students may misuse substances because of: Examination pressure Fear of failure Competition Poor academic performance Desire to stay awake or concentrate Social pressure Adults may similarly use substances because of: Long working hours Job insecurity Workplace stress Performance pressure Difficult working conditions --- slide 36 --- Lack of positive recreational activities When people have limited access to healthy activities,"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"rformance pressure Difficult working conditions --- slide 36 --- Lack of positive recreational activities When people have limited access to healthy activities, they may have more opportunities to engage in risky behaviors. Protective activities can include: Sports Music Art Community organizations Religious or cultural activities Volunteering Employment and vocational training Clubs and youth programs Such activities can provide social connection, purpose, and healthier ways of coping with stress. --- slide 36 --- Media and social media influence Movies, music, advertisements, online content, and social media can sometimes portray substance use positively or as glamorous. Repeated exposure can contribute to perceptions that: Drinking heavily is normal. Drug use is exciting. Drugs make people more attractive or confident. Substance use is an acceptable way to deal with problems. Media influence is only one factor and does not affect everyone in the same way. --- slide 36 --- Trauma, abuse, and neglect People who experience traumatic events may have an increased risk of problematic substance use. Trauma can include: Physical abuse Sexual abuse Emotional abuse Domestic violence"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ence traumatic events may have an increased risk of problematic substance use. Trauma can include: Physical abuse Sexual abuse Emotional abuse Domestic violence Childhood neglect Serious accidents War or displacement Loss of a loved one Other frightening or life-threatening experiences Drugs may be used to temporarily escape painful memories or emotions. --- slide 36 --- Poverty and unemployment Economic hardship can contribute indirectly to substance abuse. People experiencing poverty or unemployment may face: Chronic stress Hopelessness Social exclusion Poor living conditions Lack of opportunities Exposure to drug-using environments Some individuals may turn to substances as a way of coping with these circumstances. It is important to note that poverty itself does not cause drug abuse . People from all socioeconomic backgrounds can develop substance-use disorders. --- slide 36 --- Poor education and lack of awareness Some people may underestimate the risks associated with drugs. For example, they may believe: \"I can stop whenever I want.\" \"Using it once cannot hurt me.\" \"Prescription drugs are always safe.\" \"Everyone uses this substance.\" \"Addiction only happens to certain types"},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"whenever I want.\" \"Using it once cannot hurt me.\" \"Prescription drugs are always safe.\" \"Everyone uses this substance.\" \"Addiction only happens to certain types of people.\" Accurate education can help people understand the physical, psychological, social, and economic consequences of substance abuse. --- slide 36 --- Lack of parental supervision and guidance Young people who receive little supervision may have greater exposure to risky behaviors. Protective parenting generally includes: Knowing where children are and who they spend time with. Setting reasonable boundaries. Discussing drugs openly. Monitoring behavior appropriately. Providing emotional support. Establishing clear expectations. Poor supervision can make it easier for adolescents to become involved with drug-using peers."},{"u":"notes/05_drugs-and-substance-abuse-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Drugs and Substance Abuse in Kenya","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"","i":"06-hygiene-and-sanitation","x":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation Source: TOPIC 6- HYGIENE AND SANITATION NOTES.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 1 Hygiene and sanitation Hygiene Hygiene is a series of practices performed to preserve health. According to the World Health Organization (WHO), \"Hygiene refers to conditions and practices that help to maintain health and prevent the spread of diseases. Hygiene can be classified into: Community hygiene and personal hygiene. Community hygiene might include; industrial hygiene, social hygiene, food hygiene, etc. Personal Hygiene is part of hygiene, which tells us how an individual preserves, improves, and maintains the health of their own mind and body. It is taking care of yourself every day, from your hair to your feet, by following the rules of proper washing and grooming, healthful nutrition, and getting enough physical activity and rest. Personal hygiene and health are greatly affected by heredity and environment; it can be discussed in terms of constitution, posture, habit, sunshine, rest and sleep, fatigue, exercise, emotions, and cleanliness of the body, mental hygiene and nutrition. Types of hygiene. Home hygiene Home hygiene pertains to the hygiene practices that prevent or minimize the spread of disease at home and other everyday settings, such as social"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":". Home hygiene Home hygiene pertains to the hygiene practices that prevent or minimize the spread of disease at home and other everyday settings, such as social settings, public transport, the workplace, public places, etc. 2 The main \"highways\" for the spread of pathogens in the home are the hands, hand and food contact surfaces, and cleaning cloths and utensils (e.g. fecal-oral route of transmission). Pathogens can also be spread via clothing and household linens, such as towels. Safe disposal of human waste is a fundamental need; poor sanitation is a primary cause of diarrhea disease in low-income communities. Respiratory viruses and fungal spores are spread via the air. Respiratory hygiene Correct respiratory and hand hygiene when coughing and sneezing reduces the spread of pathogens, particularly during the cold and flu season. Carry tissues and use them to catch coughs and sneezes, or sneeze into your elbow. Dispose of tissues as soon as possible Hygiene in the kitchen, bathroom, and toilet Routine cleaning of hands, food, sites and surfaces (such as toilet seats and flush handles, door and tap handles, work surfaces, bath and basin surfaces) in the kitchen, bathroom and"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ds, food, sites and surfaces (such as toilet seats and flush handles, door and tap handles, work surfaces, bath and basin surfaces) in the kitchen, bathroom and toilet rooms reduces the spread of pathogens. The infection risk from flush toilets is not high, provided they are properly maintained, although some splashing and aerosol formation can occur during flushing, particularly when someone has diarrhea. Pathogens can survive in the scum or scale left behind on baths, showers, and washbasins after washing and bathing Laundry hygiene Laundry hygiene involves practices that prevent disease and its spread via soiled clothing and household linens such as towels. Items most likely to be contaminated with pathogens are those that come into direct contact with the body, e.g., underwear, personal towels, facecloths, nappies. Cloths or other fabric items used during food preparation, or for cleaning the toilet, or cleaning up material such as feces or vomit is a particular risk. 3 Oral Hygiene It is recommended that all healthy adults brush twice a day, softly, with the correct technique, replacing their toothbrush every few months (~3) or after a bout of illness. It is also not"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ealthy adults brush twice a day, softly, with the correct technique, replacing their toothbrush every few months (~3) or after a bout of illness. It is also not recommended to brush immediately after drinking acidic substances, including sparkling water. It is also recommended to floss once a day using a different piece of floss at each flossing session. Visits to a dentist for a check-up every year at least are recommended. Sleep hygiene Sleep hygiene is the recommended behavioral and environmental practice That is intended to promote better quality sleep. Sleep Hygiene 1. Establish a regular sleep schedule. 2. Not exercising physically or mentally too close to bedtime 3. limiting worry. 4. Limiting exposure to light in the hours before sleep. 5. out of bed if sleep does not come. 6. Avoiding alcohol as well as nicotine, caffeine, and other stimulants in the hours before bedtime. Personal care services hygiene Personal care services hygiene pertains to the practices related to the care and the use of instruments used in the administration of personal care services to people: Personal care hygiene practices include: Sterilization of instruments used by service providers, including"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"e administration of personal care services to people: Personal care hygiene practices include: Sterilization of instruments used by service providers, including hairdressers, aestheticians, and other service providers. Sterilization by autoclave of instruments used in body piercing and tattoo marking. Cleaning hands. 4 Personal hygiene includes: cleaning your body every day washing your hands with soap after going to the toilet brushing your teeth twice a day covering your mouth and nose with a tissue (or your sleeve) when sneezing or coughing Washing your hands after handling pets and other animals BENEFITS/IMPORTANCE OF PERSONAL HYGIENE Good hygiene is vital because it helps prevent you and your children from getting or spreading germs and infectious diseases. Conditions that you can develop if you have poor personal hygiene include: COVID-19 and other infectious diseases diarrhea , especially gastroenteritis respiratory infections, including colds and flu staph infections worm-related conditions, such as threadworms scabies - caused by a Sarcoptes scabiei var. into the skin, where it leaves eggs. Maintaining good hygiene is important to prevent scabies. trachoma, an eye"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"rms scabies - caused by a Sarcoptes scabiei var. into the skin, where it leaves eggs. Maintaining good hygiene is important to prevent scabies. trachoma, an eye infection which can lead to blindness tooth decay Washing your body Try to bathe or shower each day. Wash well, especially under your armpits and around your genitals and anus. Keeping clean will prevent skin irritations and remove bacteria that cause body odour. 5 Wash yourself with soap, shower gel or a hypoallergenic body wash. Soap removes more germs, but you may need to wash sensitive body parts with plain water or salt water. If there is no tap water or it is scarce, clean yourself with a clean wet cloth or sponge. Washing your hands To avoid getting sick, wash your hands properly for at least 20 seconds. Wet your hands with water. Apply enough soap to cover all surfaces of your hands. Rub your palms together. Clean between your fingers and the back of your hands. Clean dirty nails with a scrubbing brush, if one is available. Rinse both sides of your hands, preferably under clean running water. Dry off your hands with a clean towel. Use the towel to turn off the tap. Nail Hygiene To help prevent the spread of germs"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"preferably under clean running water. Dry off your hands with a clean towel. Use the towel to turn off the tap. Nail Hygiene To help prevent the spread of germs and nail infections: Keep nails short and trim them often. Scrub the underside of nails with soap and water (or a nail brush) every time you wash your hands. Clean any nail grooming tools before use. In commercial settings such as nail salons, external icon , sterilize nail grooming tools before use. Avoid biting or chewing nails. Avoid cutting cuticles, as they act as barriers to prevent infection. Never rip or bite a hangnail. Instead, clip it with a clean, sanitized nail trimmer Facial Hygiene Facial cleanliness is important to your health. Use soap and clean, running water to remove dirt, oil, and unwanted debris from your face. 6 Many diseases and conditions can be prevented or controlled through appropriate personal hygiene and by frequently washing parts of the face. Appropriate facial hygiene practices include not only washing the face but also properly caring for teeth, mouth, eyes, contact lenses, and ears. Pink eye (conjunctivitis) spreads easily from person to person. Good hygiene practices, such as"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"roperly caring for teeth, mouth, eyes, contact lenses, and ears. Pink eye (conjunctivitis) spreads easily from person to person. Good hygiene practices, such as handwashing, face washing, and not touching or rubbing eyes, are important for limiting the spread of pink eye. through close personal contact. It often infects entire families and communities. Poor facial hygiene can lead to the spread of this disease through eye-seeking flies and contaminated fingers. The promotion of good hygiene practices, such as handwashing and face washing at least once a day with water, is a key step in breaking the cycle of trachoma transmission. Hair hygiene (hair care) The hair follicles from which the hair grows produce oil from the sebaceous glands that keeps the hair smooth. The scalp (the skin covering the head) also has numerous sweat glands and is a surface for the accumulation of dead skin cells. The oil, sweat, and dead cells all add together and can make the hair greasy and look dirty unless you wash it regularly. Poor hair hygiene could cause dandruff and skin infections such as Tinea capitis. Dandruff is dead skin on the scalp that comes off in tiny flakes when sebaceous glands"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"r hygiene could cause dandruff and skin infections such as Tinea capitis. Dandruff is dead skin on the scalp that comes off in tiny flakes when sebaceous glands produce too much oil and accumulate on the scalp. The recommended procedures for cleaning the hair are: Use clean water to wash your hair regularly (at least twice weekly, preferably once every other day) with body soap or shampoo, whichever is available. Massage your scalp well. This will remove dead skin cells, excess oil, and dirt. Rinse well with clear water. Conditioner is helpful if you have longer hair as it makes the hair smoother and easier to Use a wide-toothed comb for wet hair as it is easier to pull through. 7 Dry the hair and the head with a clean towel. Never share a towel with someone else. Comb the hair to look beautiful for the day Menstrual hygiene (Personal hygiene for women) The vagina can clean itself; no special care is needed other than washing the external genitals. Washing the outer genital area with clean water must be a daily practice. Change tampons and sanitary napkins or pads regularly. Always wash your hands before and after handling a tampon or pad. Clean and soft cloths can be used in"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"e. Change tampons and sanitary napkins or pads regularly. Always wash your hands before and after handling a tampon or pad. Clean and soft cloths can be used in place of sanitary pads. The use of dirty clothes must be discouraged. Menstrual blood-absorbing items must be properly disposed of in a burial pit or other appropriate method SANITATION Sanitation involves providing facilities and services for the safe disposal of human urine and feces (WHO, 2018). However, from a broader perspective, the term 'sanitation' also includes maintaining hygienic conditions through city-level services. It has become clear that comprehensive sanitation solutions require an integrated approach that addresses other sectors such as solid waste management, storm water management, water supply, and access to toilets. ENVIRONMENTAL SANITATION Environmental sanitation\" means the art and science of applying sanitary, biological and physical science principles and knowledge to improve and control the environment therein for the protection of the health and welfare of the public. ENVIRONMENTAL HEALTH Promotes health, prevents disease transmission eliminates breeding places of insects Improves quality of"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"health and welfare of the public. ENVIRONMENTAL HEALTH Promotes health, prevents disease transmission eliminates breeding places of insects Improves quality of life WATER SANITATION The provision of Safe water, Sanitation and Hygiene (WASH) is crucial for human health and well-being. Water-Provision of reverse osmosis (RO) Systems and Coolers in Schools the household -In School and Community Toilet, School, community toilet complex nce of Toilets 8 g WASTE DISPOSAL Waste management (or waste disposal) includes the activities and actions required to manage waste from its inception to its final disposal. This includes the collection, transport, treatment, and disposal of waste, together with monitoring and regulation of the waste management process METHODS OF WASTE DISPOSAL (a) Dumping (b) Controlled tipping or sanitary land-fill (c) Incineration (d) Composting (e) Manure pits (f) Burial FOOD SANITATION Clean utensils and cooking equipment Separate raw and ready-to-eat foods Wash fruits and vegetables before use Use appropriate kitchen tools for food preparation A food handler should maintain hygiene and positive health Keep dry foods separate from liquids Cook food to an"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"e appropriate kitchen tools for food preparation A food handler should maintain hygiene and positive health Keep dry foods separate from liquids Cook food to an appropriate temperature Keep insects and pests away from food areas Always use clean water to prepare food Clean the kitchen and mop the floor after each food preparation Keep kitchen towels, sponges, and cleaning cloths clean and replace regularly AIR SANITATION Air pollution occurs when harmful or excessive quantities of substances are introduced into e HEALTH EFFECTS OF AIR POLLUTION Maintenance of Good Ventilation and Air ) To remove the resumed air from the buildings 2) To supply oxygen for humans in the buildings 3) To remove the excessive moisture 4) To cool the body of humans and remove the excessive heat 5) To prevent the suffocation of theatres, assembly halls, and other public meeting places. 6) To cool the surrounding objects and freshen the atmosphere inside the buildings. 9 Pollution What is Pollution? ubstances (or energy) that cause adverse changes in the Types of Pollution As stated before, there are different types of pollution, which are either caused by natural events (like forest fires) or by man-made"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"in the Types of Pollution As stated before, there are different types of pollution, which are either caused by natural events (like forest fires) or by man-made activities (like cars, factories, nuclear wastes, etc.) These are further classified into the following types of pollution: Air Pollution Water Pollution Soil Pollution Noise Pollution Besides these 4 types of pollution, other types exist such as light pollution, thermal pollution and radioactive pollution. The latter is much rarer than other types, but it is the deadliest. Air Pollution Air pollution refers to the release of harmful contaminants (chemicals, toxic gases, quite detrimental and in some cases, pose serious health issues. Some causes that contribute to air pollution are: Burning fossil fuels Mining operations Exhaust gases from industries and factories The effects of air pollution vary based on the kind of pollutant. But generally, the impact of air pollution ranges from: Increased risk of respiratory illness and cardiovascular problems Increased risk of skin diseases May increase the risk of cancer Global warming Acid rain Ozone depletion Hazards to wildlife Among the other types of pollution, air pollution"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"of skin diseases May increase the risk of cancer Global warming Acid rain Ozone depletion Hazards to wildlife Among the other types of pollution, air pollution is theorized to have a planet-wide implication. Scientists have even speculated an apocalypse-like scenario where air pollution if left unchecked, can bring about an extreme form of global warming called the runaway greenhouse effect. Though this is purely speculative, it is a phenomenon that has already occurred on Venus. 10 Water Pollution Water Pollution Water pollution is said to occur when toxic pollutants and particulate matter are introduced into water bodies such as lakes, rivers and seas. These contaminants are generally introduced by human activities like improper sewage treatment and oil spills. However, even natural processes such as eutrophication can cause water pollution. Other significant causes of water pollution include: Dumping solid wastes in water bodies Disposing untreated industrial sewage into water bodies Human and animal wastes Agricultural runoff containing pesticides and fertilisers The effects of water pollution are very pronounced in our environment. Furthermore, toxic chemicals can"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"gricultural runoff containing pesticides and fertilisers The effects of water pollution are very pronounced in our environment. Furthermore, toxic chemicals can bioaccumulate in living beings, and these chemicals can travel their way up the food chain, ultimately reaching humans. Among the other types of pollution, water pollution has severe consequences on humans. For instance, in 1932, a grave case of water pollution incapacitated the inhabitants of an entire city in Japan with neurological diseases and mental illness for many decades. However, the immediate cause was not apparent but was eventually attributed to acute mercury poisoning. Methylmercury was dumped into the surrounding bay and had ultimately bioaccumulated inside the fish. The local population then consumed these fish, and this resulted in the manifestation of ill effects and neurological diseases. Other consequences of water pollution include: Disruption of the ecosystem Threats to marine life Increased risk of water-borne diseases Increases toxic chemicals (such as mercury) in water bodies Eutrophication Soil Pollution Soil pollution, also called soil contamination, refers to the degradation of land due to the"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"als (such as mercury) in water bodies Eutrophication Soil Pollution Soil pollution, also called soil contamination, refers to the degradation of land due to the presence of chemicals or other man-made substances in the soil. The xenobiotic substances alter the natural composition of soil and affect it negatively. These can drastically impact life directly or indirectly. For instance, any toxic chemicals present in the soil will get absorbed by the plants. Since plants are producers in an environment, it gets passed up through the food chain. Compared to the other types of pollution, the effects of soil pollution are a little more obscured, but their implications are very noticeable. Some of the common causes of soil pollution are: 11 Improper industrial waste disposal Oil Spills Acid rain which is caused by air pollution Mining activities Intensive farming and agrochemicals (like fertilisers and pesticides) Industrial accidents The effects of soil pollution are numerous. Specific wastes, such as radioactive waste become particularly hazardous when they are not well-contained. A well-documented example is a nuclear accident in Chernobyl, which has left an area of 2,600 km2"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ecome particularly hazardous when they are not well-contained. A well-documented example is a nuclear accident in Chernobyl, which has left an area of 2,600 km2 uninhabitable for several thousand years. Other effects of soil pollution include: Loss of soil nutrients, which renders the soil unfit for agriculture Impacts the natural flora and fauna residing in the soil Degrades vegetation due to the increase of salinity of the soil Toxic dust (such as silica dust) can cause respiratory problems or even lung cancer Noise Pollution Noise pollution refers to the excessive amount of noise in the surroundings that disrupts the natural balance. Usually, it is man-made, though certain natural calamities like volcanoes can contribute to noise pollution. In general, any sound that is over 85 decibels is considered to be detrimental. Also, the duration an individual is exposed has an impact on their health. For perspective, a normal conversation is around 60 decibels, and a jet taking off is around 15o decibels. Consequently, noise pollution is more obvious than the other types of pollution. Noise pollution has several contributors, which include: Industry-oriented noises such as heavy"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ise pollution is more obvious than the other types of pollution. Noise pollution has several contributors, which include: Industry-oriented noises such as heavy machines, mills, factories, etc. Transportation noises from vehicles, aeroplanes, etc. Construction noises Noise from social events (loudspeakers, firecrackers, etc.) Household noises (such as mixers, TV, washing machines, etc.) Noise pollution has now become very common due to dense urbanisation and industrialisation. Noise pollution can bring about adverse effects such as: Hearing loss Tinnitus Sleeping disorders Hypertension (high BP) Communication problems"},{"u":"notes/06_hygiene-and-sanitation.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Hygiene and Sanitation","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"","i":"07-climate-change","x":"07 · Climate Change Source: Topic 7- CLIMATE CHANGE ADAPTATION AND MITIGATION notes.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 1 CLIMATE CHANGE ADAPTATION AND MITIGATION Is the climate in Earth really changing? Yes! Earth has been getting warmerand fast. Global climate is the average climate over the entire planet. And the reason scientists and folks like you are concerned is that up fastfaster than at history. What is the weather? Weather is local and temporary. Weather happens at a particular time and place. Rain, snow, wind, cyclones, hurricanes, tornadoesthese are all weather events. On our own Earth, we cannot control weather by turning the thermostat up to make it warmer or down to make it cooler. The best we can do is try to predict the weather. Weather scientists, called What is Climate? Climate is the big picture. It is the big picture of temperatures, rainfall, wind and other conditions over a larger region and a longer time than weather. For example, the weather was rainy in Turkana County, Kenya last week. But this county usually gets only about 20 cm of rain each year. So the climate for Turkana is dry. Nairobi City has a subtropical highland noticeably cooler winters. What is Climate Change? Any process that causes adjustments to a climate system could be described as creating"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"a subtropical highland noticeably cooler winters. What is Climate Change? Any process that causes adjustments to a climate system could be described as creating the phenomenon by which certain gases, so-called greenhouse gases in the atmosphere trap heat that would otherwise escape to space, thereby keeping the planet warm. and they have always caused warming. If there were no greenhouse effect, the planet would be badly cold more than 30 °C colder than the hospitable current average of 150C. 2 Greenhouse Gases: Greenhouse gases are gases that can trap heat. They get their name from greenhouses. A greenhouse is full of windows that let in sunlight. That sunlight creates warmth. The big trick at warmth escape. Carbon Dioxide (CO2): Made up of carbon and oxygen, it comes from decaying and living organisms and several other sources. CO2 is released when burning fossil fuels, coal and oil. -induced global warming. Methane (CH4): Methane, made of carbon and hydrogen, is a normal gas released from wetlands, growing rice, raising cattle, using natural gas and mining coal. It traps a lot of heat in the atmosphere, scientists consider it the second most important contributor to"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ce, raising cattle, using natural gas and mining coal. It traps a lot of heat in the atmosphere, scientists consider it the second most important contributor to human-induced global warming of all the greenhouse gases. Ozone (O3): Up in the atmosphere where the aircraft radiation, which protects us from the powerful radiation from the sun. Close to the ground ozone acts as a greenhouse gas and can be formed by burning gas in cars and factories. Nitrous Oxide (N2O): Nitrous Oxide is a natural part of the nitrogen cycle. Nitrous Oxide released by some of the factories, power plants and fertilizers. This N2O damages the protective ozone layer. ChloroFluoroCarbons (CFCs): CFCs have no natural source but were entirely synthesized for such diverse uses as refrigerants, aerosol propellants and cleaning solvents. Their creation was in 1928 and since then concentrations of CFCs in the atmosphere have been rising. So what would happen? 1. Sea level rising: Sea level rising because melting glaciers on land are adding more water to the oceans. Glaciers are large sheets of snow and ice that are found on land all year long. They are found in the western United States, Alaska, the mountains of"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"he oceans. Glaciers are large sheets of snow and ice that are found on land all year long. They are found in the western United States, Alaska, the mountains of Europe and Asia, and many other parts of the world. The giant ice sheets on Greenland and Antarctica are also considered glaciers. 3 2. Weather patterns are changing Flooding: Flooding is the most common disaster in North Africa, the second most common in East, South and Central Africa, and the third most common in West Africa. 2. Changes in Water Supply and Quality Observable effects of climate change on water resources in Africa include: flooding, drought, change in distribution of rainfall, drying-up of rivers, melting of glaciers and the receding of some lakes. 3. Food productivity Droughts, heat stress and flooding have led to a reduction in crop yields and livestock productivity. 4. Impacts on Human Health Climate-sensitive diseases and health impacts can be high in poor countries that have minimal resources to treat and prevent illness. Examples of climate related health impacts include: Frequent and severe heat stress linked to sustained increases in temperature. The spread of Malaria may increase in areas"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"limate related health impacts include: Frequent and severe heat stress linked to sustained increases in temperature. The spread of Malaria may increase in areas projected to receive more precipitation and flooding. Increases in rainfall and temperature can cause spreading of dengue fever. 5. Impact on shelter Severe flooding and intense droughts have led to the destruction of many homes, shelters and villages across Africa. Conflicts over resources also exacerbate these impacts and, in turn, contribute to the ongoing migration within and between countries in Africa. 6. Impacts on Vulnerable Population Women, children and the elderly are more vulnerable to climate change impacts across Africa. Women often experience additional duties as caregivers and as well as from societal responses to climate change after extreme weather events (eg, male migration). The water scarcity places an additional burden on African women, who walk hours and sometimes even days, to fetch it. 7. Impacts on the ecosystem The migration patterns, geographic range and seasonal activity of many terrestrial and marine species have shifted in response to climate change. 4 8. Impacts on National Security Climate"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"graphic range and seasonal activity of many terrestrial and marine species have shifted in response to climate change. 4 8. Impacts on National Security Climate change impacts have the potential to exacerbate national security issues and increase the number of international conflicts. Conflicts often occur over the use of already limited natural resources, fertile ground and water. Access to consistent and dependable sources of water is greatly valued in many African regions. CLIMATE CHANGE ADAPTATION AND MITIGATION Two strategies exist to address climate change: adaptation and mitigation Climate change Adaptation This refers to how species change their behaviour and bodies to better fit in their natural environment. Types of adaptation i. Physiological adaptation These are the mechanisms within an organism to enable it carry out specific biochemical reaction for survival in its natural ecosystem. lity to produce acid for digestion. ii. Behavioral adaptation This entails specific ways that an organism does for survival such as migration, aestivation, and hibernation iii. Structural adaptations These are certain features involving certain part of an shape. Climate Change Mitigation"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"as migration, aestivation, and hibernation iii. Structural adaptations These are certain features involving certain part of an shape. Climate Change Mitigation These are the efforts to control global warming and associated impacts. It mainly entails controlling the emission of Greenhouse gases and eliminating their remnants in the atmosphere. 5 Strategies to Mitigate Climate Change 1. Carbon sequestration Carbon sequestration, also known as carbon capture and storage (CCS) entails technologies aimed at capturing CO2 emissions from power plants, industrial facilities and storing it underground for an indefinite period of time. This prolonged storage is aimed at delaying global warming. 2. Carbon sink It is a human-made reservoir that gather and retain carbon-containing compounds from the atmosphere, thereby reducing the amount of CO2 in the atmosphere. The two global carbon sinks are the ocean and the planted vegetation. These two have been emphasized since the signing of the Kyoto Protocol. 3. Carbon Credit This is a permit that authorizes the holder to release a SPECIFIC amount of CO2 and other greenhouse gases. Each credit permits the emission of 1 metric ton of CO2 or its"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"permit that authorizes the holder to release a SPECIFIC amount of CO2 and other greenhouse gases. Each credit permits the emission of 1 metric ton of CO2 or its equivalent in other gases. Entities with emission allowances are allowed to emit up to a defined limit, which is progressively reduced over time. Subsequently, companies are allowed to sell any unused credit to other companies that may need it. If one exceeds their credit, they have to subscribe to more, and they can get incentives by selling carbon credits that they have in surplus. 4. Carbon Tax This is a levy imposed on companies that produce, distribute, and utilize fossil fuels, determined by the quantity of carbon generated during combustion. This promotes research into renewable energy source, leaving the fossil fuels. 5. Geo-engineering These are the techniques employed to help in cooling the atmosphere such as: a. Deploying parasols in space, b. Positioning mirrors in the orbit, 6 c. Using sulfate aerosols to brighten the stratosphere d. Applying reflective coating to building roofs e. Introducing iron fillings into the ocean to stimulate the growth of carbon-absorbing algae. Role of learning institutions in"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ective coating to building roofs e. Introducing iron fillings into the ocean to stimulate the growth of carbon-absorbing algae. Role of learning institutions in combating climate change 1. Vulnerability Assessment: The students from the school form a team of Climate Change Crusaders (CCC) to carry out participatory climate change vulnerability assessment within their community. Such an assessment should address issues such as: a) The main hazards or dangers in your area? b) Events of drought, flood, conflict between various user community, changes in river course, human-wildlife conflict, water availability. c) What are the main impacts of these hazards in the community? d) Within your understanding, what will be the future scenario of your area? (drought/flood forced migration). e) Who will be the most affected within the community? f) What will be the status of forests, grasslands, wetlands?2. g) What action do you suggest from your experience? 2. Reforestation: The CCC need to organise reforestation campaigns with all relevant stakeholders in the community to reforest the degraded or deforested areas with native species. For the benefit of the community and generate livelihood"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"elevant stakeholders in the community to reforest the degraded or deforested areas with native species. For the benefit of the community and generate livelihood from the regenerated forests in-between plant acacias which could be cut down to make charcoal, which could provide economic incentive to local community to protect the regenerated forests. 3. Protecting and restoring riverbank/lakes: Riverbanks control flooding and are habitats for many special plants and animals. Protecting and restoring riverbanks / lake bunds help in water and soil conservation and provide a habitat for pollinators for our crops. 4. Prescribe crop variety and rotation: Discuss with farmers in your area and find out what are the crops they are planting. Suggest to farmers to plant a mixture of food crops and cash crops, traditional crops and new varieties. In this way, some of the crops are likely to survive drought or flood. Some of the traditional food crops are well adapted to dry seasons. 6. Planting trees at school and at home: Trees provide shade, hold the soil, clean the air and can later be used for wood. Planting alone is not enough, however; the young trees need to be cared for. 7. Selling"},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"vide shade, hold the soil, clean the air and can later be used for wood. Planting alone is not enough, however; the young trees need to be cared for. 7. Selling livestock before a drought: Encourage herders to sell some livestock each season. This brings in income. A smaller livestock herd will need less food during the dry season. The animals are more likely to survive than those in a large herd."},{"u":"notes/07_climate-change.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Climate Change","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"","i":"08-digital-environment","x":"08 · Digital Environment Source: TOPIC 8 DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes CCS 105: HEALTH, ENVIRONMENT, AND SOCIETY TOPIC 8: DIGITAL ENVIRONMENT https://www.genevaenvironmentnetwork.org/resources/updates/data-digital-technology-and-the-environment/  A virtual place accessible through the connectedness of the Internet.  An online space created by digital technologies, platforms and networks through which people communicate, access information, learn, work, and interact. Focal point Social media presence Digital footprints Use of AI Scamming & digital fraud Core components of a digital environment  Digital devices(hardware): Physical electronic equipment to access, process, store and share digital information.  Internet & connectivity (networks): Infrastructure & technologies that enable devices to connect and exchange information. Social media: Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn Search engines: Google, Microsoft Bing Learning: Google Classroom, Moodle and Microsoft Teams Communication: WhatsApp, Telegram, Zoom Productivity: Microsoft Office (Word, Excel), Google Workspace E-commerce: Jumia, Kilimall, Amazon  Digital platforms & applications (software): Programs & online platforms that enable users to perform different activities;"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"-commerce: Jumia, Kilimall, Amazon  Digital platforms & applications (software): Programs & online platforms that enable users to perform different activities; communication, information sharing, learning, entertainment. E.g., Digital devices: Computers (desktops and laptops), smartphones, tablets, smart TVs, smartwatches, digital cameras Internet & connectivity: Wi-Fi networks, mobile networks (3G, 4G, 5G), fibre-optic internet connections, Bluetooth connectivity, satellite internet service Core components of a digital environment  Digital content & information : Different forms of information created, stored and shared electronically. E.g., Text documents, e-books, electronic reports Online newspapers, blogs, digital publications Digital photographs, graphics, illustrations Digital maps, databases Videos, podcasts, audio recordings Statistical datasets  Users & digital communities : Individuals and groups who participate in digital environments and interact through digital platforms. E.g., Students attending online classes Online discussion groups and communities Lecturers sharing learning materials through Moodle Content creators, bloggers Professionals collaborating through"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"sses Online discussion groups and communities Lecturers sharing learning materials through Moodle Content creators, bloggers Professionals collaborating through LinkedIn Digital entrepreneurs Core components of a digital environment  Security & privacy systems: Tools and practices used to protect devices, information, accounts and online identities. E.g., Artificial Intelligence (AI): ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Microsoft Copilot Cloud computing: Google Drive, Microsoft OneDrive, Dropbox Internet of Things (IoT): Smart home devices, connected agricultural sensors, wearable health monitors Big data: Analysis of large datasets to identify patterns and support decision-making Virtual & augmented reality (VR/AR): Virtual learning environments, interactive simulations. Blockchain: Distributed digital records used in areas such as financial transactions and supply-chain tracking Passwords & multi-factor authentication (MFA) Firewalls & encryption Antivirus & anti-malware software Virtual Private Networks (VPNs) Privacy settings on social media platforms Data backup & recovery systems  Emerging technologies : Advanced technologies that are transforming how people interact with digital"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"on social media platforms Data backup & recovery systems  Emerging technologies : Advanced technologies that are transforming how people interact with digital systems & information. Examples of digital environments Digital environments are part of everyday life: we learn, work, communicate, interact, & have fun in the digital age! Other examples of digital environments Social and communication WhatsApp, Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn, Telegram, and X. E-commerce and Financial Services Jumia, Amazon, M-Pesa, mobile banking, online shopping and digital payment platforms Healthcare and Wellness Telemedicine platforms, online medical consultations, electronic health records, fitness applications and health- monitoring devices. Government and Public Services eCitizen Kenya, KRA iTax, online passport applications, digital licensing and public information portals. Research and Information Google Scholar, ResearchGate, PubMed, digital repositories, online databases and electronic journals News and Media Online newspapers, digital news platforms, blogs, podcasts and online broadcasting services. Travel and Navigation Google Maps, Uber, Bolt, online flight booking, hotel"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ewspapers, digital news platforms, blogs, podcasts and online broadcasting services. Travel and Navigation Google Maps, Uber, Bolt, online flight booking, hotel reservation platforms and GPS applications. Social media presence  Refers to how an individual, organization or institution is represented and interacts with others on social media platforms.  For university students, this involves how you create profiles, share information, communicate and participate in online communities.  It includes the information shared through profiles, posts, comments, photos, videos, likes, follows, and other online interactions.  Also includes the groups and communities joined, and the way an individual communicates with others.  Your social media presence contributes to your digital identity and reputation. What you share, how you interact and how you conduct yourself online can have lasting implications for your personal, academic, and professional life. Key aspects of social media presence  Online identity: How you present yourself through your profile, username, profile picture, biography and personal information.  Content sharing: Information, photographs, videos, opinions, and"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"yourself through your profile, username, profile picture, biography and personal information.  Content sharing: Information, photographs, videos, opinions, and comments that you post or share on social media.  Online interactions : How you communicate and engage with others through likes, comments, messages, discussions, and sharing of posts.  Digital reputation: The impression others develop about you based on your online activities, behaviour, and shared content.  Privacy and security : Managing account privacy settings and protecting personal information from unauthorized access or misuse. Social media presence  Different platforms may serve different purposes. Consider the type of information and content you share on each platform. Platform Common use Snapchat Sharing photos & short videos, instant messaging, video calls Facebook Social networking and sharing information Instagram Sharing photographs, videos and stories TikTok Creating and sharing short videos WhatsApp Messaging and group communication X (Twitter) Sharing opinions, news and public discussions LinkedIn Professional networking and career development Responsible social media presence Risks associated with"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"r) Sharing opinions, news and public discussions LinkedIn Professional networking and career development Responsible social media presence Risks associated with social media  Cyberbullying & online harassment: Exposure to insults, threats, intimidation, humiliation or abusive messages from other users.  Privacy breaches & identity theft : Personal information, photographs and account details may be exposed, stolen or misused.  Online scams & fraud: Fraudsters may use fake accounts, phishing links, fraudulent scholarship offers and deceptive promotions to steal money or personal information.  Misinformation & disinformation:Exposure to or sharing of false, misleading or manipulated information. Risks associated with social media  Reputational damage : Inappropriate posts, comments, photographs or videos may negatively affect one's academic, personal and future professional reputation.  Social media addiction & reduced productivity: Excessive use may interfere with academic work, concentration, sleep and other daily responsibilities.  Mental health & social pressure: Social comparison, negative comments and pressure to gain likes and followers may contribute to stress,"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ily responsibilities.  Mental health & social pressure: Social comparison, negative comments and pressure to gain likes and followers may contribute to stress, anxiety and reduced self-esteem.  Exposure to inappropriate content: Encountering harmful, violent, sexually explicit or misleading material. Digital footprints  Every time you go online, you leave a trail.  There is a collection of information about you that exists on the internet.  A digital footprint is the unique trail of data and information you leave behind every time you use the internet and digital platforms.  May include social media posts, comments and reactions, photos and videos, online searches, websites visited, online purchases, emails and messages, and information provided when registering for online services. Types of digital footprints  Two main types of digital footprints: active and passive Why your digital footprint matter  Personal reputation & relationships: Online activities influence how others perceive. Online posts and interactions can affect relationships with friends, colleagues and family.  Academic & career opportunities : Your online presence may influence scholarship and educational"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"can affect relationships with friends, colleagues and family.  Academic & career opportunities : Your online presence may influence scholarship and educational opportunities. Employers may review publicly available online information when assessing job applicants  Digital advertising: Online activities build personalized advertisements and recommendations.  Privacy & security: Exposed personal information promote identity theft, fraud & privacy breaches.  Permanence: Information online can be saved, cached, or resurface years later even after you think it has been deleted. Artificial intelligence (AI)  AI refers to the ability of computer systems to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence, such as learning, problem- solving, recognizing patterns, understanding language, and assisting with decision-making. Essentials:  AI literacy  Digital literacy and security  Critical thinking  Information verification  Ethical AI use Artificial intelligence (AI): Application Examples AI chatbots ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Microsoft Copilot, Claude, Grok, DeepSeek Virtual assistants Google Assistant, Apple Siri, Amazon Alexa, Claude, ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Microsoft"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"oogle Gemini, Microsoft Copilot, Claude, Grok, DeepSeek Virtual assistants Google Assistant, Apple Siri, Amazon Alexa, Claude, ChatGPT, Google Gemini, Microsoft Copilot Image generation DALL·E, Adobe Firefly Recommendation systems YouTube, Netflix, Spotify, Amazon Translation Google Translate, DeepL, Microsoft Translator Navigation Google Maps route predictions Data analysis Data preparation, predictive modelling Scamming and digital fraud  These are dishonest activities carried out through digital platforms or electronic communication to deceive people into revealing personal information, transferring money or providing access to their accounts.  A scam is a deceptive activity designed to trick people into giving away money, personal information, access to accounts or other valuable resources.  Scammers commonly use email, SMS, phone calls, social media, messaging applications, fake websites, and online marketplaces. Scamming and digital fraud  Digital fraud involves using digital technologies to deliberately deceive individuals or organizations for financial or other unlawful gain.  Any deceptive activity that uses the internet, computers, or mobile devices to steal money,"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"individuals or organizations for financial or other unlawful gain.  Any deceptive activity that uses the internet, computers, or mobile devices to steal money, personal data, or sensitive information from individuals and businesses.  In Kenya and globally, these threats grow fast as criminal networks use AI, social media ads, and fast mobile payment systems. Warning signs  Unsolicited links or attachments, weird spelling in website URLs, or generic greetings.  Pressure to act immediately or send money via non-reversible channels.  Requests for remote access to your phone or computer.  Requests for passwords & PINs.  Unverified investment or employment opportunities.  Offers that look \"too good to be true,\" e.g., massive job pay, quick crypto gains, or goods sold far below market value. Protecting yourself in the digital environment: A simple approach is to STOP – CHECK – ACT.  STOP: Do not immediately respond to suspicious messages, click links or send money.  CHECK: Verify: 1) Who sent the message? 2) Is the request genuine? 3) Is the website or account legitimate? Is the information supported by reliable sources?  ACT: Take appropriate action. What to do if you are"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"equest genuine? 3) Is the website or account legitimate? Is the information supported by reliable sources?  ACT: Take appropriate action. What to do if you are scammed?  Contact your bank or mobile network operator immediately to try and freeze or reverse the transaction.  Report the incident e.g., bank's customer support line or dedicated reporting email.  Change your passwords and multi-factor authentication settings. Key message  As university students, it is important to develop digital competence and digital citizenship.  Digital competence is the ability to confidently, effectively and critically use digital technologies to access, evaluate, create and share information, communicate, solve problems, and adapt to emerging technologies such as artificial intelligence  Digital citizenship means using digital technologies responsibly, ethically, safely, and respectfully. It involves respecting others online, protecting personal information, observing digital rights and responsibilities, and contributing positively to online communities --END--"},{"u":"notes/08_digital-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Digital Environment","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"","i":"09-national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment","x":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment Source: TOPIC 9 NATIONAL VALUES AND KENYAN POLITICAL ENVIRONMENT.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes TOPIC 9: NATIONAL V ALUES AND KENYAN POLITICAL ENVIRONMENT Values are important and lasting beliefs, standards, ethics or ideals shared by members of the same community, same culture, same society, same nation or same country. Values are morals and standards of what is good or bad, and what is desirable or undesir able. Values have a major influence on a person's behaviour and attitude and serve as broad guidelines in an individual's conduct in all situations. In Kenya, we are guided by values found in Article 10 of the Constitution. The national values and principles of governance in Article 10(1) of the constitution bind all State organs, State officers, public officers and all persons whenever any of them. Apply or interpret the Constitution. Enact, apply or interpret any law. Make or implement public policy decisions. National values National values are beliefs of a nation guiding the attitudes, actions and behavior of its citizens. Principles of Governance These are normative principles that oblige the state to perform its functions in a manner that promotes the general well-being of the people. Article 10(2) of the constitution highlights the following National"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ate to perform its functions in a manner that promotes the general well-being of the people. Article 10(2) of the constitution highlights the following National Values and Principles of Governance Patriotism (Uzalendo) Patriotism refers to love, loyalty and devotion shown to one's country. As patriotic Kenyans we should love our motherland by paying taxes, protecting our national resources, working hard to build Kenya. We should be Proud of our brand Kenya and protect it. National Unity (Umoja wa Kitaifa) National Unity is a cohesion which results from shared values, vision purpose and aspirations irrespective of the ethnic, cultural, economic, religious or any other super visual status in a unitary state, while recogniz ing diversity. It is a choice to work as a team in tackling the challenges that face us as a nation. “One Kenya, One People, One Destiny”. Sharing and Devolution of Power (ugavi na ugatuzi wa mamlaka) Sharing and Devolution of Power is a governance environment in which power, political, economic and social resources are distributed between the national and county levels of government. Rule of Law (Utawala wa Sheria) Rule of law Refers to the legal maxim that each"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"resources are distributed between the national and county levels of government. Rule of Law (Utawala wa Sheria) Rule of law Refers to the legal maxim that each citizen and person within the state should respect and observe the law. We must all observe the law for an orderly society. Democracy and Participation of the People (Demokrasia na Ushirika wa Watu) Democracy is a political culture in which all citizens exercise direct and equal participation in the running of the affairs of their country. Participation is the practice of involving all people in making decisions which ensures mutual respect and encourages shared responsibilities. Human dignity (Hadhi ya Binadamu) Human dignity refers to the honor bestowed on individuals, people and the upholding of their rights as spelt out in the Constitution and the Bill of Rights regardless of one’s status. Equity (Usawa) Equity refers to fairness, impartiality, justice and provision of equal access to national resources and opportunities regardless of gender, disability, age, race, ethnicity, religion or status. Social Justice (Haki ya Jamii) Social Justice Refers to the enjoyment of the rights that enhance the wellbeing of an"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":", age, race, ethnicity, religion or status. Social Justice (Haki ya Jamii) Social Justice Refers to the enjoyment of the rights that enhance the wellbeing of an individual in all aspects of their interaction with one another and promotes solidarity. It is the fairness in the service delivery, dis tribution of wealth, opportunities, and privileges within a society by the Government. “Huduma Bora Ni Haki Yako”. Inclusiveness (Uhusishwaji) Inclusiveness refers to according all people an opportunity to participate in activities including decision making, that affect their life. Equality (Ulingano) Equality Refers to treatment of everyone with equal measure irrespective of their status such as gender, religion, social class, tribe or race. It also refers to being regarded in equal status in all matters involving rights of individuals and people groups. Human Rights (Haki za Binadamu) Human rights refers to God given inherent inalienable liberties and entitlements to be enjoyed by all people and upholding of their rights as spelt out in the constitution. They include: The right to life, freedom of exp ression, right to health, housing, free from hunger, social security, education, the"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"lt out in the constitution. They include: The right to life, freedom of exp ression, right to health, housing, free from hunger, social security, education, the right to development and self-determination. Non-discrimination (Kutobagua) Non discrimination refers to offering services and taking care of all persons without bias. Protection of the marginalized (Kulinda Kundi Lililotelekezwa) Protection of the marginalized refers to safeguarding livelihood of those who have challenges or those that do not have a voice in society. National Cohesion and Integration Act No. 12 of 2 008 outlaws marginalization. Equalization Fund (Article 204), YEF, Uwezo Funds, AGPO are some of the key initiatives in Kenya to address the issue. Good Governance (Utawala Bora) Good Governance Refers to having systems and structures through which the exercise of power and authority can be controlled or held to account. It also refers to the prudent management of institutions and resources by tho se entrusted with the responsibility of overseeing them. Integrity (Uadilifu) Integrity refers to adherence to virtuous moral and ethical principles. This includes honesty, truthfulness, consistency and moral"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ng them. Integrity (Uadilifu) Integrity refers to adherence to virtuous moral and ethical principles. This includes honesty, truthfulness, consistency and moral character both in private and public life. It also refers to the quality of being steadfast and consistent about what is right. Transparency and Accountability (Uwazi na Uwajibikaji) Transparency refers to the requirement to share information and act in an open manner. Accountability refers to being held responsible for one’s actions, conduct and management of resources. Sustainable Development (Maendeleo ya Kudumu) Sustainable development refers to the pattern of resource use that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. Political context Kenya’s political context has been heavily shaped by historical domestic tensions and contestation associated with centralisation and abuse of power, high levels of corruption, a more than two decades long process of constitutional review and post-election violence. The approval of the new constitution in 2010 and relatively peaceful elections in March 2013 are milestones constituting steps forward in Kenya’s"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"tion violence. The approval of the new constitution in 2010 and relatively peaceful elections in March 2013 are milestones constituting steps forward in Kenya’s transition from political crisis. Government and Political System The Republic of Kenya is a unitary State. Kenya won its independence from British colonial rule on 12 December 1963. The country has a multi -party political system whose hallmark is parliamentary democracy. The Parliament of Kenya is bicameral house consisting of the National Assembly and the Senate. The National Assembly has in total 349 members plus the Speaker who is an ex-officio. Article 95 of the Constitution establishes that the National Assembly shall consist of the following:- two hundred and ninety (290) members, each elected by the registered voters of single member constituencies; forty -seven (47) women, each elected by the registered voters of the counties, each county constituting a single member constituency; twelve (12) members nominated by p arliamentary political parties according to their proportion of members of the National Assembly in accordance with Article 90, to represent special interests including the youth, persons with"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ccording to their proportion of members of the National Assembly in accordance with Article 90, to represent special interests including the youth, persons with disabilities and workers; and the Speaker, who is an ex officio member. The Senate consists of 67 members plus the Speaker, who is an ex -officio member. Article 98 of the Constitution establishes that the Senate shall consist of the following: - Forty-seven (47) members each elected by the registered voters of the counties, eac h county constituting a single member constituency; Sixteen (16) women members who shall be nominated by political parties according to their proportion of members of the Senate elected under clause (a) in accordance with Article 90; Two members (2), being one man and one woman, representing the youth; Two (2) members, being one man and one woman, representing persons with disabilities; and The Speaker, who shall be an ex officio member. The members of the National Assembly are referred as Members of Parliament (MP) while the Members of the Senate are referred as Senators. Both the MPs and Senators serve a 5-year term. The President of the Republic of Kenya, together with the Deputy -President and"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Senate are referred as Senators. Both the MPs and Senators serve a 5-year term. The President of the Republic of Kenya, together with the Deputy -President and the Cabinet Secretaries comprise the executive. The President shall nominate and, with the approval of the National Assembly, appoint Cabinet Secretaries. A Cabinet Secretary sh all not be a Member of Parliament. The President is elected directly for a 5 -year term. In order to win, a candidate must garner 50% plus 1 votes and 25% in half of 47 counties. Parliamentary politics in Kenya is open, free, fair and highly competitive field. Kenya has indeed held all its general elections – presidential, parliamentary, and local authorities every 5 years as required by the Constitution, without fail since the cou ntry attained independence in 1963. On March 4, 2013, Kenya went to a general election, the first ever under the new constitution which was promulgated in August, 2010 The electoral system and political parties By at least some standards, Kenya has consolidated an election culture. Since independence, Kenya has held regular and periodic elections roughly every five years. The principle of free and fair elections is"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ted an election culture. Since independence, Kenya has held regular and periodic elections roughly every five years. The principle of free and fair elections is legally enshrined in Kenya’s constitution and provided for in the Elections Act and the Political Parties Act. In addition, Kenya has ratified many international and regional treaties that contain standards on the conduct of democratic elections. It has signed, but not yet ratified, the African Union’s Charter on Democracy, Elections and Governance. Although Kenya has consolidated a culture of elections, the electoral system of first -pastthe-post has prevented the institutionalisation of democracy and political parties. This echoes the findings of the Independent Review Commission (IREC), later referred to as the Kriegler Commission after its chairman Justice Johann Kriegler, on the general election of 2007, which found that Kenya’s electoral system was not founded on the principle of the equality of votes. The system had been distorted for a long while through gerrymandering. The Kriegler Commission pointed out that the system contained gaps and weaknesses that warranted radical review in order to provide free and fair"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"errymandering. The Kriegler Commission pointed out that the system contained gaps and weaknesses that warranted radical review in order to provide free and fair elections.16 Elections are won or lost by a simple majority, regardless of the number of registered voters in a constituency that cast their ballots. This system reinforces ethnicity by privileging the mobilisation of communities through discourses that emphasise the need to access and control state power for the benefit of those elites that win the presidency. For these reasons, parties are formed and reformed along ethno-regional lines as vehicles for electoral competition only, causing people to associate any new government with the community of the president. The majoritarian electoral system thus makes rivalry for power a constant element in Kenya’s political life. Because winning an election using the first - past-the-post system requires mobilising sufficient numbers, elites enter into ethnic coalitions using the numeric strength of their respective communities as a bargaining chip. This leads to a cycle of formation and break up of ethnic coalitions every election period. Many of these alliances break up after the"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ies as a bargaining chip. This leads to a cycle of formation and break up of ethnic coalitions every election period. Many of these alliances break up after the election and few live beyond one election. The new constitution has not radically altered the electoral system. Although there were discussions about introducing Proportional Representation (PR) or Mixed Member Proportional Representation (MMPR), the constitution generally retained the first-past-the-post system, because MPs did not want to give up representing their constituencies. PR and the MMPR electoral systems in particular, would have strengthened democracy in several ways. It would have assisted in laying a foundation for stronger political parties as institutions, because people would be voting for political parties rather than their ethnic leaders. MMPR would have weakened ethnicity as a basis for political organising and representation by ensuring that membership of parties is national and that the parties have a membership that represents the diverse interests and groups that make up Kenya Concentration of power and abuse of the rule of law Successive governments have manipulated the constitution to concentrate"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"erests and groups that make up Kenya Concentration of power and abuse of the rule of law Successive governments have manipulated the constitution to concentrate more powers in the presidency. Amendments to the constitution began at independence in 1963 in order to create a powerful ‘imperial presidency’ by combining the powers of the queen (head of state) and those of the Prime Minister (head of government). The amendments sought to create a powerful president, who was both the head of state and head of government. These powers were centralised and concentrated in the presidency but with minimal checks. With absolute powers, the first president, Jomo Kenyatta, and the cabal of ethnic elites around him, began to amend other provisions of the constitution. Amendments were made to dismantle the se mi-federal system of government and later on the bi -cameral legislature was abolished. In other instances the government would use administrative fiat to cause changes in the practice of politics. For instance, from 1969 the government did not allow opposi tion political parties to operate. This practice of politics and increased appetite to concentrate power in the executive was passed on"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ent did not allow opposi tion political parties to operate. This practice of politics and increased appetite to concentrate power in the executive was passed on to the regime of President Moi who assumed office after Kenyatta’s death in 1978. Constitutional amendments were made to constrain dissent and suppress viewpoints that differed from those of the government and the ruling elites in KANU. The amendments to concentrate power in the executive watered down the foundational principles and values of the Independence Constitution and undermined participatory democracy. In the end, the amendments resulted in a dominant executive. The amendments also eroded the independence of other organs, the legislature and the judiciary. They also evolved the executive into the dominant player in the policy -making arena. Furthermore, influential politicians had an overwhelming impact in the policy arena. Their informal power was so overwhelming that they would subjugate the government ministries/departments in policy -making. Political influence took primacy over everything else and undermined the capacity of state institutions to make policies Even within the executive, it became increasingly"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"influence took primacy over everything else and undermined the capacity of state institutions to make policies Even within the executive, it became increasingly difficult to independently formulate policies, because everything had to be referred to the 22 PART I KENYA: Democracy and Political Participation president or be sanctioned by him. Civil servants, who delayed implementing the different directives or questioned them, were punished. The first few years of President Kibaki’s rule saw a radical departure from these approaches. During this time, before the fragmentation of the ruling coalition, the government expanded the space for policy-making to include civil society and non-state actors in general. The government would use an inclusive and participatory approach to policy -making. Thus, civil society organisations (CSOs) participated in the drafting of the Economic Recovery Strategy (ERS), the policy framework that NARC used to guide planning and national development. CSOs also contributed to the development of a framework for governance and justice reforms immediat ely after the new government came to power. So much so that the new government’s policies borrowed the"},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"f a framework for governance and justice reforms immediat ely after the new government came to power. So much so that the new government’s policies borrowed the language and thinking of civil society and other pro -reform groups. But the collapse of the alliance saw a return to the tendency by government to consul t minimally with civil society. The collapse of the alliance meant the government could not without effort deliver on its legislative agenda in Parliament. The government had to look for new allies, which included political parties that were initially part of the opposition and individua l politicians who had limited or no interest in pursuing the radical reforms that NARC had promised to undertake. With these new allies, NARC reduced its interest in governance reforms. This had immediate consequences. The momentum for reforms dissipated as the government had no interest in any reform that could hurt its quest to maintain adequate numbers in Parliament."},{"u":"notes/09_national-values-and-kenyan-political-environment.html","g":"Notes","t":"09 · National Values and Kenyan Political Environment","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"","i":"10-unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya","x":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya Source: Topic 10_UNEMPLOYMENT AND POVERTY MITIGATION IN KENYA.txt. This page is searchable, and the checklist state is saved in this browser."},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Learning checks","i":"learning-checks","x":"Learning checks Identify the key terms and explain them without looking. Summarize the main idea in your own words. Work through an example or answer a related question."},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes TOPIC 10: UNEMPLOYMENT AND POVERTY MITIGATION IN KENYA UNEMPLOYMENT Unemployment remains one of the major socio-economic challenges facing many developing countries, including Kenya. The youth, who make up over 60% of Kenya’s population, are the most affected. However, they also represent the greatest potential resource for addressing the unemployment crisis through creativity, innovation, and entrepreneurship. Expected learning outcomes By the end of this lesson, students should be able to: Explain the meaning and causes of unemployment among youth. Discuss strategies for promoting youth participation in job creation. Appreciate the importance of skills development and innovation in reducing unemployment. Definition: Unemployment refers to the situation where people who are willing and able to work cannot find jobs. There are several types of unemployment, each with different causes and characteristics. The main types are discussed below: Frictional Unemployment This occurs when people are temporarily between jobs or are entering the workforce for the first time. It’s often short-term and happens because of the time it takes to find a suitable job. Example: A recent"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"jobs or are entering the workforce for the first time. It’s often short-term and happens because of the time it takes to find a suitable job. Example: A recent college graduate looking for their first job or someone who quit their job to find a better one. Structural Unemployment Happens when there is a mismatch between the skills workers have and the skills needed by employers. Often caused by technological changes, changes in consumer demand, or shifts in the economy. Can be long-term because workers may need retraining. Example: Factory workers losing jobs because of automation. Cyclical Unemployment Caused by economic downturns or recessions. When demand for goods and services decreases, businesses reduce production and lay off workers. It fluctuates with the business cycle. Example: Layoffs during a recession when companies face reduced sales. Seasonal Unemployment Occurs when people are unemployed at certain times of the year because their jobs are seasonal. Common in industries like agriculture, tourism, and retail. Example: Farm workers unemployed after harvest season or holiday retail workers after the season ends. Classical (or Real-Wage) Unemployment Happens when wages"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Example: Farm workers unemployed after harvest season or holiday retail workers after the season ends. Classical (or Real-Wage) Unemployment Happens when wages are kept above the market-clearing level, causing excess labor supply. Often due to minimum wage laws, labor unions, or other wage rigidities. Example: Workers willing to work for lower wages can’t find jobs because wages are fixed too high. main causes of unemployment explained clearly: Economic Downturns (Recessions) When the economy slows down, businesses sell fewer goods and services. To cut costs, companies lay off workers, causing cyclical unemployment. Example: During a recession, factories reduce production and workers lose jobs. Technological Changes Automation and new technologies can replace human labor. Workers whose skills become outdated may lose their jobs, leading to structural unemployment. Example: Assembly line workers replaced by robots. Mismatch of Skills Sometimes the skills workers have don’t match the skills employers need. This happens when industries change or new industries emerge. Result: People remain unemployed because they need retraining or education. Example: Coal miners losing jobs as the"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"industries change or new industries emerge. Result: People remain unemployed because they need retraining or education. Example: Coal miners losing jobs as the economy shifts to renewable energy. Seasonal Changes Certain industries only need workers during specific seasons. Off-season, these workers are temporarily unemployed. Example: Ski instructors or agricultural workers during off-peak times. Government Policies Minimum wage laws, labor unions, and unemployment benefits can sometimes discourage hiring or reduce the incentive to work. Example: If minimum wages are set too high, some employers may hire fewer workers. Lack of Job Creation If the economy isn’t growing enough, there won’t be enough new jobs for people entering the workforce. This causes higher unemployment, especially for young people. Population Growth Rapid growth in the working-age population can increase unemployment if job creation doesn’t keep up. More people are competing for the same number of jobs. Globalization Jobs may move to countries with cheaper labor, causing unemployment in some industries in other countries. Example: Manufacturing jobs moving from developed to developing countries. effective"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"cheaper labor, causing unemployment in some industries in other countries. Example: Manufacturing jobs moving from developed to developing countries. effective ways to reduce unemployment: Promoting Economic Growth When the economy grows, businesses expand and create more jobs. Governments can boost growth through investments in infrastructure, supporting industries, and encouraging entrepreneurship. Improving Education and Training Providing education and vocational training helps workers gain the skills employers need. This reduces structural unemployment by matching workers’ skills to job market demands. Encouraging Labor Market Flexibility Making it easier for businesses to hire and fire workers can reduce classical unemployment. This can include reforming labor laws or reducing excessive regulations. Supporting Small and Medium Enterprises (SMEs) SMEs are often major job creators. Governments can provide financial support, reduce taxes, or simplify business registration to help SMEs grow and hire more people. Implementing Active Labor Market Policies Programs like job counseling, job matching services, and subsidies for hiring the unemployed can help people find jobs faster."},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ting Active Labor Market Policies Programs like job counseling, job matching services, and subsidies for hiring the unemployed can help people find jobs faster. Example: Public job fairs or online job portals. Encouraging Technological Adaptation and Retraining Helping workers adapt to new technologies through retraining programs reduces structural unemployment. Governments and businesses can partner to offer these training opportunities. Reducing Seasonal Unemployment Diversifying the economy so there are year-round job opportunities in more sectors. Encouraging tourism or agriculture innovations that extend the working season. Monetary and Fiscal Policies Central banks can lower interest rates to encourage borrowing and investment. Governments can increase spending or cut taxes to stimulate demand and create jobs, especially during recessions. Encouraging Entrepreneurship Supporting startups and self-employment can generate new jobs. This can be done through training, grants, and easing access to credit. Role of Youth in Addressing Unemployment Introduction Main Content a) Entrepreneurship and Innovation Youth can become job creators rather than job seekers by starting small and"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Addressing Unemployment Introduction Main Content a) Entrepreneurship and Innovation Youth can become job creators rather than job seekers by starting small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs). Innovation and technology (e.g., digital startups, e-commerce, agritech) can generate new forms of employment. Government initiatives like the Ajira Digital Program and Hustler Fund and nyota fund encourage youth entrepreneurship. b) Skills Development and Education Youth should actively pursue vocational and technical training through TVET institutions. Continuous upskilling and reskilling ensures they remain relevant to emerging industries (ICT, renewable energy, data analysis). Partnerships between schools, industries, and government can bridge the skills gap. c) Community and Cooperative Development Youth can engage in community-based projects such as agriculture, waste management, and eco-tourism. Formation of youth cooperatives enables collective investment and employment creation at local levels. d) Advocacy and Policy Participation Youth should participate in policy dialogues and national development forums. Through youth councils, NGOs, and online platforms, they can advocate for:"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ipation Youth should participate in policy dialogues and national development forums. Through youth councils, NGOs, and online platforms, they can advocate for: Fair employment policies. Youth-inclusive budgeting. Internship and apprenticeship programs. e) Digital Economy Participation The digital sector offers vast opportunities for online work, content creation, freelancing, and digital marketing. Youth can leverage platforms such as Upwork, Fiverr, and Ajira Digital to earn income remotely. ICT skills open access to global employment opportunities. f) Promoting Sustainable and Green Jobs Youth can engage in climate-smart agriculture, renewable energy, and environmental conservation. These areas provide employment while contributing to sustainable development. g) Networking, Mentorship, and Volunteering Youth should build professional networks and join mentorship programs to gain exposure. Volunteering enhances experience, discipline, and employability. Collaboration with private sector and NGOs can expand access to job markets. Challenges Facing Youth in Addressing Unemployment Limited access to finance and credit. Inadequate entrepreneurial skills. Mismatch between education"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"markets. Challenges Facing Youth in Addressing Unemployment Limited access to finance and credit. Inadequate entrepreneurial skills. Mismatch between education and labor market needs. Lack of mentorship and exposure. Policy implementation gaps. Strategies to Strengthen Youth Involvement Expand funding for youth enterprises. Support vocational and digital skills programs. Encourage innovation hubs and incubation centers. Create enabling environments for startups and informal businesses. Strengthen partnerships between government, private sector, and educational institutions. Conclusion The youth are a vital force in combating unemployment through their energy, creativity, and innovation. When empowered with skills, resources, and opportunities, they can drive economic growth, job creation, and social transformation. Sustainable solutions to unemployment must therefore place youth at the center of national development strategies. References International Labour Organization (ILO). (2023). Global Employment Trends for Youth 2023. Geneva: ILO. Government of Kenya. (2024). Kenya Youth Development Policy (2019–2024). Ministry of ICT, Innovation & Youth Affairs. The World Bank. (2023)."},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"2023. Geneva: ILO. Government of Kenya. (2024). Kenya Youth Development Policy (2019–2024). Ministry of ICT, Innovation & Youth Affairs. The World Bank. (2023). Youth Employment in Sub-Saharan Africa. Washington, DC: World Bank. Trading Economics. (2024). Kenya Unemployment Rate. Retrieved from https://tradingeconomics.com/kenya/unemployment-rate The Star Kenya. (2025). Skills Gap Fueling Unemployment in Kenya. Retrieved from https://www.the-star.co.ke Assignment: Discuss the following questions “Youth are not the problem of unemployment but the solution.” Discuss this statement in relation to the role of young people in Kenya’s socio-economic development. Evaluate the challenges faced by youth in starting and sustaining businesses in Kenya. Propose five practical strategies that can enhance youth participation in job creation and self-employment. Assess the impact of technology and innovation on youth employment opportunities in Kenya. THE BIG QUESTION?? CAN UNEMPLOYMENT LEAD TO POVERTY??? POVERTY Introduction: Poverty is a complex and multidimensional social, economic, and political phenomenon. It refers to a condition in which individuals or groups lack the financial resources"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"a complex and multidimensional social, economic, and political phenomenon. It refers to a condition in which individuals or groups lack the financial resources and essentials for a minimum standard of living. Poverty affects health, education, access to basic services, and participation in society. Understanding poverty helps in formulating policies that promote social justice, economic equality, and sustainable development. Learning Objectives By the end of this lecture, students should be able to: Define and explain the concept of poverty. Identify the different types and dimensions of poverty. Understand the causes and effects of poverty. Examine strategies for poverty reduction and eradication. Discuss global and national initiatives aimed at poverty alleviation. What is Poverty? Poverty is generally defined as a state of deprivation where people lack access to basic human needs such as food, clean water, shelter, healthcare, and education. Key Definitions: World Bank (2022): Poverty is living on less than US$2.15 per day (international poverty line). UN (2015): Poverty is the denial of choices and opportunities, a violation of human dignity. It means lack of basic capacity"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"r day (international poverty line). UN (2015): Poverty is the denial of choices and opportunities, a violation of human dignity. It means lack of basic capacity to participate effectively in society. Absolute Poverty: When individuals cannot meet the minimum level of income required for basic living. Relative Poverty: When individuals’ income or resources are significantly less than the average within a society, leading to social exclusion. Types of Poverty Absolute Poverty: Lack of basic necessities such as food, water, shelter, and healthcare. Relative Poverty: Exists when individuals have less income compared to others in the same society. Situational Poverty: Caused by a sudden crisis or loss (e.g., natural disasters, job loss). Chronic Poverty: Persistent poverty that continues over generations. Urban Poverty: Found in metropolitan areas, often linked to slum living conditions. Rural Poverty: Associated with lack of infrastructure, employment opportunities, and access to services in rural areas. Multidimensional Poverty: Includes not only income but also lack of education, health, and living standards. Causes of Poverty Poverty results from multiple, interconnected factors"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"erty: Includes not only income but also lack of education, health, and living standards. Causes of Poverty Poverty results from multiple, interconnected factors including: Economic Causes: Unemployment and underemployment Low wages and unequal distribution of income Economic recessions and inflation Social Causes: Poor access to education and healthcare Gender inequality Discrimination based on ethnicity, race, or class Political Causes: Corruption and poor governance Political instability and conflict Lack of inclusive economic policies Environmental Causes: Droughts, floods, and environmental degradation Climate change impacts on agriculture Structural Causes: Unequal land ownership Historical injustices (e.g., colonial legacies) Weak institutional frameworks Effects of Poverty Health: Malnutrition, disease, and limited access to healthcare. Education: Low enrollment and high dropout rates. Economic: Low productivity and limited economic growth. Social: Crime, insecurity, and social exclusion. Political: Disenfranchisement and political instability. Environmental: Overexploitation of natural resources due to survival needs. Measurement of Poverty Common tools used to measure"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"ement and political instability. Environmental: Overexploitation of natural resources due to survival needs. Measurement of Poverty Common tools used to measure poverty include: Poverty Line: The minimum income required to meet basic needs. Headcount Index: Percentage of the population living below the poverty line. Poverty Gap Index: Measures the intensity of poverty. Human Development Index (HDI): Composite measure including income, education, and health. Multidimensional Poverty Index (MPI): Developed by UNDP to assess multiple deprivations at once. Strategies for Poverty Reduction Economic Empowerment: Job creation, entrepreneurship, and microfinance. Education and Skills Training: Improving literacy and vocational skills. Healthcare Access: Ensuring affordable healthcare services. Social Protection Programs: Cash transfers, pensions, and food subsidies. Agricultural Development: Promoting sustainable farming and access to markets. Good Governance: Transparency and equitable resource distribution. Infrastructure Development: Improving transport, energy, and communication systems. Environmental Sustainability: Supporting climate-resilient livelihoods. Global and National"},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Development: Improving transport, energy, and communication systems. Environmental Sustainability: Supporting climate-resilient livelihoods. Global and National Efforts United Nations Sustainable Development Goal 1 (SDG 1): End poverty in all its forms everywhere by 2030. World Bank Poverty Reduction Strategy Papers (PRSPs): National frameworks for poverty reduction. Kenya Vision 2030: Aims to transform Kenya into a middle-income country with a high quality of life. National Social Protection Policy (Kenya): Supports vulnerable populations through cash transfer programs. Conclusion Poverty is not merely a lack of income but a multidimensional issue involving social, economic, political, and environmental factors. Addressing poverty requires comprehensive and inclusive strategies focusing on equity, empowerment, and sustainable development."},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"cal, and environmental factors. Addressing poverty requires comprehensive and inclusive strategies focusing on equity, empowerment, and sustainable development. "},{"u":"notes/10_unemployment-and-poverty-mitigation-in-kenya.html","g":"Notes","t":"10 · Unemployment and Poverty Mitigation in Kenya","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Worked solution — reveal it step by step 1 Retrieval practice 2 Step 2 Close or cover the notes. Write the key idea, method, or definitions you remember. 3 Step 3 Reopen the notes and correct your recall in a different color. Add weak points to the progress tracker. Reveal all steps Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"Question bank","h":"","i":"question-bank","x":"Question bank Use these prompts after studying a topic. Write your answer before searching the notes; then record what you missed in the progress tracker. Define the key terms introduced in each topic. Explain the central idea of each topic in your own words. Compare two related terms, processes, or methods from the course. Reproduce one worked example without referring to the source. Apply one idea from the course to a new example or context. Identify one misconception or error that could lose marks. Revisit every question you could not answer confidently."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"Answer key and self-assessment","h":"","i":"answer-key-and-self-assessment","x":"Answer key and self-assessment This pack is based on the provided course outline and lecture material. For open-ended questions, check the relevant topic note and the original source material. A complete answer should use accurate terminology, show the reasoning or method, and address every part of the prompt. Ready: correct explanation without notes. Review: mostly correct, but needed a prompt or missed an important step. Retry: could not explain the idea yet; revisit the note and try again. Keep corrections in the weak-topic action plan rather than copying answers without understanding them."}];